content:
# Big-O notation

**Big-O** tells you how the running time (or memory) **grows** as the input size `n` grows. It ignores small details and constants.

**Analogy:** finding a friend's name.
- **O(1):** you know their seat number, so you go straight there.
- **O(log n):** a phone book. Open the middle and throw away half each time.
- **O(n):** check every person one by one.
- **O(n²):** compare every person with every other person (a handshake at a party).

| Big-O | Name | n = 1,000 → steps (roughly) |
|---|---|---|
| O(1) | constant | 1 |
| O(log n) | logarithmic | 10 |
| O(n) | linear | 1,000 |
| O(n log n) | linearithmic | 10,000 |
| O(n²) | quadratic | 1,000,000 |
| O(2ⁿ) | exponential | astronomically large |

```java
// O(n): one loop over the array
int sum(int[] a) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int x : a) total += x;
    return total;
}

// O(n²): a loop inside a loop
boolean hasDuplicate(int[] a) {
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
        for (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++)
            if (a[i] == a[j]) return true;
    return false;
}
```

**Line by line**
- `sum`: the loop visits each element once. Double the array and you double the work, so it is **O(n)**.
- `hasDuplicate`: for each `i`, the inner loop visits the remaining elements. That is about n×n/2 comparisons, and we drop constants, so it is **O(n²)**.
- `j = i + 1`: we start after `i` so that we don't compare an element with itself or compare the same pair twice.

**Rules of thumb**
- Drop constants: O(2n) → O(n).
- Keep the biggest term: O(n² + n) → O(n²).
- Nested loops multiply. Loops one after another add.
- Halving each step gives log n.

Q: What is the time complexity of finding an item in a sorted array using binary search?
A: O(log n), because each step throws away half of the remaining items.

# Stack: balanced parentheses

A **stack** is **LIFO: Last In, First Out**. **Analogy:** a stack of plates. You put plates on top (`push`) and take from the top (`pop`).

A **queue** is **FIFO: First In, First Out**, like a line at a bank counter.

In Java, use `ArrayDeque` for both (the old `Stack` class is synchronized and slow).

## Problem: is "{[()]}" balanced?

**Idea:** every closing bracket must match the **most recent** unmatched opening bracket. "Most recent" is exactly what a stack gives you.

```java
boolean isBalanced(String s) {
    Deque<Character> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        if (c == '(' || c == '{' || c == '[') {
            st.push(c);                       // opening: remember it
        } else {
            if (st.isEmpty()) return false;   // closing with nothing open
            char open = st.pop();             // the latest opening bracket
            if ((c == ')' && open != '(') ||
                (c == '}' && open != '{') ||
                (c == ']' && open != '[')) {
                return false;                 // wrong type of bracket
            }
        }
    }
    return st.isEmpty();                      // leftovers mean unclosed brackets
}
```

**Line by line**
- `Deque<Character> st = new ArrayDeque<>()`: our stack. `Deque` means "double-ended queue". Here we use only one end.
- `s.toCharArray()`: lets us loop over each character.
- `push(c)`: put an opening bracket on top.
- `st.isEmpty()` check: for `")("`, the first `)` has no partner.
- `pop()`: removes and returns the top bracket, which must match.
- `return st.isEmpty()`: for `"(("`, the loop ends with brackets still open, so the string is not balanced.

**Trace "{[()]}":** push { → push [ → push ( → `)` pops ( ✓ → `]` pops [ ✓ → `}` pops { ✓ → stack empty → **true**.

Q: Why not use `java.util.Stack`?
A: It extends `Vector`, so every method is synchronized (slow) and it exposes index access. `ArrayDeque` is faster and is the recommended stack in modern Java.

# Trees and traversals

A **tree** is a structure of nodes in which each node has children, like a family tree or a folder structure.
A **binary tree** gives each node at most 2 children (left, right).
In a **BST (Binary Search Tree)**: left < node < right.

```java
class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left, right;
    TreeNode(int v) { val = v; }
}
```

## The four traversals (for a tree with root 2, left 1, right 3)

| Traversal | Order | Result | Used for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Inorder | Left, Root, Right | 1 2 3 | Sorted output of a BST |
| Preorder | Root, Left, Right | 2 1 3 | Copy / serialize a tree |
| Postorder | Left, Right, Root | 1 3 2 | Delete a tree (children first) |
| Level order | Level by level | 2 1 3 | BFS, shortest path |

**Memory trick:** "pre / in / post" tells you **where the Root goes**: before, in the middle, or after.

```java
void inorder(TreeNode n) {
    if (n == null) return;      // base case: empty subtree
    inorder(n.left);            // 1. everything smaller
    System.out.print(n.val + " ");  // 2. this node
    inorder(n.right);           // 3. everything bigger
}

int height(TreeNode n) {
    return n == null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(height(n.left), height(n.right));
}
```

**Line by line**
- `if (n == null) return;`: **every recursive function needs a base case**, or it never stops.
- `inorder`: for a BST this prints values in sorted order, because left values are smaller and right values are bigger.
- `height`: an empty tree has height 0. Otherwise it is 1 (this node) plus the taller of the two subtrees.

## Level order (BFS) with a queue

```java
List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
    if (root == null) return res;
    Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
    q.add(root);
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int size = q.size();                 // nodes on the current level
        List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            TreeNode n = q.poll();           // take from the front
            level.add(n.val);
            if (n.left != null) q.add(n.left);    // children wait for the next level
            if (n.right != null) q.add(n.right);
        }
        res.add(level);
    }
    return res;
}
```

**Line by line**
- A **queue** (FIFO) processes nodes in the order they were found, which naturally goes level by level.
- `int size = q.size()`: we freeze the count **before** the loop. The children we add during the loop belong to the next level and must not be counted now.
- `poll()`: removes the front node. `add()`: puts children at the back.

**Analogy:** a school assembly. Call the first row, and as each student comes up, their "children" join the back of the line.

Q: Which traversal gives sorted output for a BST?
A: Inorder (Left, Root, Right).

# Graphs: BFS and DFS

A **graph** is a set of nodes (vertices) connected by edges, like cities joined by roads.
We usually store it as an **adjacency list**: `Map<Integer, List<Integer>>` (each city → its neighbouring cities).

## BFS (Breadth-First Search)
**Analogy:** a ripple in a pond. First visit all neighbours 1 step away, then 2 steps away, and so on.

```java
void bfs(Map<Integer, List<Integer>> g, int start) {
    Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>(List.of(start));
    Queue<Integer> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(start));
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int u = q.poll();                        // next node to process
        System.out.println(u);
        for (int v : g.getOrDefault(u, List.of())) {
            if (seen.add(v)) q.add(v);           // add only if never seen
        }
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `seen`: stops us visiting a node twice. Without it, a cycle (A→B→A) would loop forever.
- `seen.add(v)` returns **true only if v was new**. That one call does "check and mark" together, which is a neat trick.
- `getOrDefault(u, List.of())`: a node with no edges gives an empty list instead of null.

## DFS (Depth-First Search)
**Analogy:** exploring a maze. Follow one path as deep as possible, and turn back only at a dead end.

```java
void dfs(Map<Integer, List<Integer>> g, int u, Set<Integer> seen) {
    if (!seen.add(u)) return;                    // already visited
    System.out.println(u);
    for (int v : g.getOrDefault(u, List.of())) dfs(g, v, seen);
}
```

**Line by line**
- Recursion uses the **call stack** as its stack, so DFS goes deep before wide.
- `if (!seen.add(u)) return;`: visit each node only once.

| | BFS | DFS |
|---|---|---|
| Uses | Queue | Stack / recursion |
| Good for | Shortest path (unweighted), levels | Cycle detection, topological sort, connected components |

**Topological sort:** orders tasks so that each task comes after the tasks it depends on (e.g. "install Java" before "run Maven"). Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly take the nodes with **in-degree 0** (no remaining dependencies) from a queue.

Q: Which traversal finds the shortest path in an unweighted graph?
A: BFS, because it explores nodes in order of their distance from the start.

# Sorting complexity cheat sheet

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Space | Stable |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Bubble sort | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |
| Insertion sort | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | Yes |
| Selection sort | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(1) | No |
| Merge sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Yes |
| Quick sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(log n) | No |
| Heap sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(1) | No |
| Binary search | O(1) | O(log n) | O(log n) | O(1) | - |

**Stable** means equal items keep their original order. Example: sort students by grade, and students with the same grade stay in alphabetical order if they were alphabetical before.

**Simple analogies**
- **Bubble sort:** the biggest bubbles float to the top one by one.
- **Insertion sort:** how you sort playing cards in your hand. Pick the next card and slide it into the right spot.
- **Selection sort:** repeatedly pick the smallest item and put it at the front.
- **Merge sort:** split the pile in half, sort each half, then merge the two sorted halves.
- **Quick sort:** pick a pivot, put smaller items on the left and bigger on the right, then repeat on each side.

```java
// Bubble sort: easy to write by hand
void bubbleSort(int[] a) {
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
        boolean swapped = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
                int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;   // swap
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        if (!swapped) break;      // already sorted, so stop early (best case O(n))
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- The outer loop `i` counts passes. After each pass the largest remaining item is at the end.
- `a.length - 1 - i`: the last `i` items are already in place, so we skip them.
- The swap uses a temporary variable `t`, otherwise one value would be overwritten.
- `swapped` flag: if a whole pass makes no swap, the array is sorted. That is why the best case is O(n).

**Java tip:** `Arrays.sort(int[])` uses dual-pivot Quicksort (primitives). `Arrays.sort(Object[])` and `Collections.sort` use **TimSort** (stable, a merge-sort hybrid).

Q: Which sort would you choose for guaranteed O(n log n) with stability?
A: Merge sort (TimSort in Java).

# Two pointers and sliding window

These patterns replace a slow "check every pair" O(n²) solution with a single pass, O(n).

## Sliding window: longest substring without repeating characters

**Idea:** keep a "window" `[left, right]` that holds no duplicates. Move `right` forward. When a repeated character appears, jump `left` past its previous position.

**Analogy:** a train window moving along a row of houses. You always look at a stretch with no repeated house colour.

```java
int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
    Map<Character, Integer> last = new HashMap<>(); // char -> last index seen
    int best = 0, left = 0;
    for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
        char c = s.charAt(right);
        if (last.containsKey(c) && last.get(c) >= left) {
            left = last.get(c) + 1;      // shrink: start after the old copy
        }
        last.put(c, right);              // remember the latest position
        best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
    }
    return best;
}
```

**Line by line**
- `last`: remembers where we last saw each character.
- `last.get(c) >= left`: the old copy only matters if it is **inside** the current window. If it is before `left`, it is already outside.
- `left = last.get(c) + 1`: move the window start just past the duplicate.
- `right - left + 1`: the window length (+1 because both ends are included).
- Example "abcabcbb" → 3 ("abc").

## Two Sum with a HashMap: O(n)

```java
int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> idx = new HashMap<>();   // value -> index
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        Integer j = idx.get(target - nums[i]);     // the partner we need
        if (j != null) return new int[]{j, i};
        idx.put(nums[i], i);
    }
    return new int[0];
}
```

**Line by line**
- For each number, ask: "Have I already seen the number that completes the target?" That partner is `target - nums[i]`.
- `Integer j` (not `int`): `get` returns `null` when the key is missing, and an `int` cannot be null.
- We `put` **after** checking so that a number is not paired with itself.

## Two pointers on a sorted array

```java
boolean pairWithSum(int[] sorted, int target) {
    int i = 0, j = sorted.length - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        int s = sorted[i] + sorted[j];
        if (s == target) return true;
        if (s < target) i++; else j--;   // need bigger -> move left pointer right
    }
    return false;
}
```
- Start at both ends. If the sum is too small, the only way to grow it is to move `i` right. If it is too big, move `j` left.

# Dynamic programming (DP)

**Idea:** if a problem breaks into smaller problems that **repeat**, solve each small problem once and **save the answer**.

**Analogy:** someone asks you 1+1+1+1+1 = ? You count: 5. They add "+1" and you say 6 straight away. You didn't recount; you **remembered** the 5. That is DP.

DP needs two things:
- **Overlapping subproblems:** the same small problem is solved many times.
- **Optimal substructure:** the best answer is built from the best answers of the subproblems.

Two styles:
- **Memoization (top-down):** recursion + a cache.
- **Tabulation (bottom-up):** fill a table with a loop, from small to big.

## Fibonacci, both ways

```java
// Top-down: memoization
long fib(int n, long[] memo) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];          // already solved
    return memo[n] = fib(n - 1, memo) + fib(n - 2, memo);
}

// Bottom-up: tabulation with O(1) space
long fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    long a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) { long c = a + b; a = b; b = c; }
    return b;
}
```
- Plain recursion recomputes fib(3) many times, which is O(2ⁿ). With memo, each value is computed once: O(n).

## Coin change: the fewest coins to make an amount

```java
int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {
    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];        // dp[a] = min coins for amount a
    Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);           // "infinity" (impossible) marker
    dp[0] = 0;                             // 0 coins make amount 0
    for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++)
        for (int c : coins)
            if (c <= a)
                dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);
    return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
}
```

**Line by line**
- `dp[a]`: the answer for the smaller amount `a`. We build up from 0 to `amount`.
- `amount + 1` acts as infinity, because you can never need more than `amount` coins (using coins of 1).
- `dp[a - c] + 1`: "use one coin `c`, then solve the rest (a − c), which we already know".
- At the end, if the value is still "infinity", the amount is impossible, so return -1.
- Example: coins [1,2,5], amount 11 → 3 (5+5+1).

# Common handwritten problems

These are the most common "write a function" questions. Practise each one **on paper**.

```java
boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
    int i = 0, j = s.length() - 1;
    while (i < j)
        if (s.charAt(i++) != s.charAt(j--)) return false;
    return true;
}
```
- Two pointers move from both ends toward the middle. "madam" → true. `i++` / `j--` move the pointers after the comparison.

```java
boolean isPrime(int n) {
    if (n < 2) return false;
    for (int i = 2; (long) i * i <= n; i++)
        if (n % i == 0) return false;
    return true;
}
```
- Only check up to √n: if n = a × b, one of a or b must be ≤ √n.
- `(long) i * i`: prevents overflow when i is large.

```java
long factorial(int n) {
    return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial(n - 1);
}
```
- Recursion: 5! = 5 × 4!. The base case `n <= 1` returns 1. Use `long` because 13! already overflows `int`.

```java
boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
    if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
    int[] cnt = new int[26];                  // one counter per letter a-z
    for (int i = 0; i < a.length(); i++) {
        cnt[a.charAt(i) - 'a']++;             // count up for a
        cnt[b.charAt(i) - 'a']--;             // count down for b
    }
    for (int c : cnt) if (c != 0) return false;
    return true;
}
```
- `charAt(i) - 'a'`: turns 'a'..'z' into 0..25 (a char is really a number).
- If both words use exactly the same letters, every counter ends at 0. "listen"/"silent" → true.

```java
Map<Character, Long> charFrequency(String s) {
    return s.chars()
            .mapToObj(c -> (char) c)
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(c -> c, LinkedHashMap::new, Collectors.counting()));
}
```
- `s.chars()` gives an `IntStream` of character codes, and `(char) c` turns each back into a Character.
- `groupingBy(key, mapFactory, counting())`: groups equal characters and counts them. `LinkedHashMap` keeps first-seen order.

```java
void fizzBuzz(int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        System.out.println(i % 15 == 0 ? "FizzBuzz"
                         : i % 3 == 0 ? "Fizz"
                         : i % 5 == 0 ? "Buzz"
                         : String.valueOf(i));
}
```
- Check **15 first**: a multiple of 15 is also a multiple of 3, so testing 3 first would print "Fizz" by mistake.

```java
String reverse(String s) {
    return new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
}
int secondLargest(int[] a) {
    int first = Integer.MIN_VALUE, second = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
    for (int x : a) {
        if (x > first) { second = first; first = x; }
        else if (x > second && x != first) second = x;
    }
    return second;
}
```
- `secondLargest`: one pass. When a new maximum appears, the old maximum becomes second. `x != first` skips duplicates of the maximum.

# The four pillars of OOP

| Pillar | Simple meaning | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| **Encapsulation** | Hide data, expose safe methods | An ATM: you can't touch the cash, only use the buttons |
| **Abstraction** | Show *what*, hide *how* | A car's steering wheel: you don't need to know the engine |
| **Inheritance** | A child class reuses a parent class (IS-A) | A dog IS-AN animal |
| **Polymorphism** | One interface, many forms | "Speak" makes a dog bark and a cat meow |

```java
// Encapsulation: balance is private and can only change through rules
public class Account {
    private BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.signum() <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        balance = balance.add(amount);
    }
    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
}

// Abstraction + Inheritance + Polymorphism
abstract class Animal {
    abstract String sound();                 // WHAT (no HOW here)
    void speak() { System.out.println(sound()); }
}
class Dog extends Animal { String sound() { return "Woof"; } }
class Cat extends Animal { String sound() { return "Meow"; } }

Animal a = new Dog();
a.speak();       // "Woof": decided at runtime (dynamic dispatch)
```

**Line by line**
- `private BigDecimal balance`: nobody outside can write `account.balance = -500`. That is encapsulation.
- `deposit` validates first: the class **protects its own rules** (invariants).
- `abstract String sound()`: every animal must make a sound, but the parent doesn't say which. That is abstraction.
- `Dog extends Animal`: Dog inherits `speak()` for free. That is inheritance.
- `Animal a = new Dog()`: the variable type is Animal, but the object is a Dog. Java calls `Dog.sound()` at runtime. That is **runtime polymorphism (overriding)**.

## Two kinds of polymorphism
- **Compile-time (overloading):** same method name, different parameters. `print(int)`, `print(String)`.
- **Runtime (overriding):** a subclass redefines a parent method. Chosen at runtime by the actual object type.

Q: Overloading vs overriding?
A: Overloading means the same name with different parameters in the same class, resolved at compile time. Overriding means a subclass gives a new body to an inherited method with the same signature, resolved at runtime.

# SOLID principles

| Letter | Principle | Simple meaning |
|---|---|---|
| **S** | Single Responsibility | One class, one job, one reason to change |
| **O** | Open/Closed | Add new behaviour by **adding** code, not editing old code |
| **L** | Liskov Substitution | A child must work wherever the parent is expected |
| **I** | Interface Segregation | Many small interfaces beat one fat interface |
| **D** | Dependency Inversion | Depend on interfaces, not concrete classes |

**Analogies**
- **S:** a chef cooks and a cashier takes money. If the chef also does accounts, a tax rule change would affect the kitchen.
- **O:** a power strip. To add a new device you plug it in; you don't rewire the strip.
- **L:** if a "Square extends Rectangle" breaks `setWidth()` (because the height changes too), it is not a true substitute.
- **I:** a printer interface shouldn't force a simple printer to implement `fax()` and `scan()`.
- **D:** a lamp depends on "a socket" (an interface), not on one specific power plant.

## Open/Closed + Strategy pattern: interest calculation

```java
public interface InterestCalculator {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal principal, int months);
}

@Component("flat")
class FlatInterest implements InterestCalculator {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal p, int m) {
        return p.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.10"))          // 10% per year
                .multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(m))
                .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(12), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}

@Component("declining")
class DecliningInterest implements InterestCalculator {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal p, int m) { /* different formula */ return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}

@Service
class LoanService {
    private final Map<String, InterestCalculator> calculators;

    LoanService(Map<String, InterestCalculator> calculators) {   // Spring injects ALL implementations
        this.calculators = calculators;
    }
    BigDecimal interest(String method, BigDecimal p, int m) {
        return calculators.get(method).calculate(p, m);
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `InterestCalculator`: the **abstraction** that LoanService depends on (D).
- `@Component("flat")`: registers this class as a Spring bean named "flat".
- `new BigDecimal("0.10")`: **use the String constructor**. `new BigDecimal(0.1)` carries the binary float error.
- `divide(..., 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP)`: money needs 2 decimals and an explicit rounding rule. Without a scale, `divide` can throw `ArithmeticException` for non-terminating decimals.
- `Map<String, InterestCalculator>`: Spring fills the map with **bean name → bean**.
- To add a "compound" method, you **add a new class**. LoanService never changes. That is Open/Closed (O).

Q: Give a real Spring example of Dependency Inversion.
A: Constructor injection of an interface. The service depends on `PaymentGateway`, and Spring decides which implementation to plug in.

# Design patterns

A **design pattern** is a proven solution to a common design problem, like a standard recipe every chef knows.

| Pattern | Type | Purpose | Java / Spring example |
|---|---|---|---|
| Singleton | Creational | Exactly one instance | Spring beans (default scope) |
| Factory | Creational | Hide object creation | `BeanFactory`, `Calendar.getInstance()` |
| Builder | Creational | Build complex objects step by step | Lombok `@Builder`, `StringBuilder` |
| Adapter | Structural | Convert one interface to another | `HandlerAdapter` in Spring MVC |
| Decorator | Structural | Add behaviour by wrapping | `BufferedInputStream` |
| Proxy | Structural | Control access / add behaviour | Spring AOP, `@Transactional` |
| Facade | Structural | Simple front for a complex system | The service layer |
| Strategy | Behavioral | Swap algorithms at runtime | `Comparator`, `InterestCalculator` |
| Observer | Behavioral | Notify subscribers | `ApplicationEventPublisher`, `@EventListener` |
| Template Method | Behavioral | Fixed skeleton, variable steps | `JdbcTemplate` |
| Chain of Responsibility | Behavioral | Pass a request along handlers | Servlet filters, the Spring Security filter chain |

## Singleton (thread-safe, lazy: the holder idiom)

```java
public class Config {
    private Config() {}                                  // nobody else can call new
    private static class Holder {
        static final Config INSTANCE = new Config();     // created on first use
    }
    public static Config getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `private Config()`: a private constructor blocks `new Config()` from outside.
- `Holder` is a nested class. The JVM loads it **only when `getInstance()` is first called**, so creation is lazy.
- Class loading in Java is thread-safe, so we get one instance with no `synchronized` needed.
- **Analogy:** a country has one president. Everyone asks "who is the president?" and gets the same person.

## Builder

```java
public class Loan {
    private final String memberId;
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final int months;

    private Loan(Builder b) { memberId = b.memberId; amount = b.amount; months = b.months; }

    public static class Builder {
        private String memberId; private BigDecimal amount; private int months = 12;
        public Builder memberId(String v) { memberId = v; return this; }
        public Builder amount(BigDecimal v) { amount = v; return this; }
        public Builder months(int v) { months = v; return this; }
        public Loan build() { return new Loan(this); }
    }
}
Loan loan = new Loan.Builder().memberId("M1").amount(new BigDecimal("50000")).build();
```
- Each setter `return this`, so calls can be **chained**.
- It avoids a constructor with 10 parameters where you mix up the order. Fields can stay `final` (immutable).
- `months = 12`: a default value when the caller doesn't set it.
- **Analogy:** ordering a burger: "bun, then patty, then cheese, then build".

## Observer with Spring events

```java
public record LoanApproved(Long loanId) {}

@Service
class LoanService {
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher events;
    LoanService(ApplicationEventPublisher events) { this.events = events; }
    void approve(Long id) { /* ... */ events.publishEvent(new LoanApproved(id)); }
}

@Component
class SmsNotifier {
    @EventListener
    void on(LoanApproved e) { System.out.println("Send SMS for loan " + e.loanId()); }
}
```
- LoanService doesn't know who is listening. New listeners (email, audit) can be added without touching it.
- **Analogy:** a YouTube channel. The creator uploads once, and all subscribers are notified.

# equals() and hashCode()

- `==` checks whether two references point to the **same object** (same address).
- `equals()` checks whether two objects are **logically equal** (same content), if you override it.
- `hashCode()` returns an int used by HashMap/HashSet to pick a bucket.

**The contract:** if `a.equals(b)` is true, then `a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()` **must** be true. (The reverse is not required: two different objects may share a hash.)

**Analogy:** hashCode is the **shelf number** in a library, and equals is checking the **exact title** on that shelf. If two copies of the same book were put on different shelves, you would never find the second one.

```java
public class Member {
    private final String nationalId;
    private final String name;

    public Member(String nationalId, String name) {
        this.nationalId = nationalId;
        this.name = name;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;                       // same object
        if (!(o instanceof Member other)) return false;   // null or a different type
        return nationalId.equals(other.nationalId);       // business identity
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(nationalId);                  // use the SAME fields as equals
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `this == o`: a quick shortcut. An object always equals itself.
- `o instanceof Member other`: (Java 16+ pattern matching) checks the type and casts in one step. `instanceof` is false for `null`, so null is handled too.
- We compare `nationalId` only, because two Member objects with the same national ID represent the same person.
- `Objects.hash(nationalId)`: builds the hash from **the same fields** used in `equals`.

## What breaks if you override only equals?

```java
Set<Member> set = new HashSet<>();
set.add(new Member("123", "Rahim"));
set.contains(new Member("123", "Rahim"));  // false if hashCode is NOT overridden!
```
- Without `hashCode`, each object gets a different default hash (based on identity), so the lookup goes to the wrong bucket and never even calls `equals`.

**Tip:** a Java `record` generates correct `equals`/`hashCode` automatically. Avoid using mutable fields in `hashCode`: if the field changes while the object is inside a HashSet, the object gets "lost".

# Exceptions

An **exception** is an event that breaks normal flow: a file is missing, the network is down, input is bad.

```
Throwable
├── Error            (JVM problems: OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError — don't catch)
└── Exception
    ├── IOException, SQLException ...   (CHECKED: must handle or declare)
    └── RuntimeException                 (UNCHECKED: programming bugs)
        ├── NullPointerException
        ├── IllegalArgumentException
        └── ArithmeticException ...
```

| | Checked | Unchecked |
|---|---|---|
| Checked at compile time | Yes, you must `catch` or `throws` | No |
| Typical cause | External problems (file, network) | Bugs, bad input |
| Example | `IOException` | `NullPointerException` |

**Analogy:** a checked exception is like the weather forecast saying "it may rain", and the compiler forces you to carry an umbrella (handle it). An unchecked exception is tripping over your own shoelace: a mistake you should fix, not plan for.

## try / catch / finally and try-with-resources

```java
// Old style
BufferedReader r = null;
try {
    r = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"));
    System.out.println(r.readLine());
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.err.println("Could not read: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    if (r != null) try { r.close(); } catch (IOException ignored) {}
}

// Modern: try-with-resources (Java 7+)
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
} catch (IOException e) {
    System.err.println("Could not read: " + e.getMessage());
}
```

**Line by line**
- `try`: the code that may fail.
- `catch (IOException e)`: runs only if that type of exception (or a subclass) is thrown.
- `finally`: **always** runs, whether or not there was an exception. It is for cleanup.
- `try (resource)`: anything that implements `AutoCloseable` is **closed automatically** at the end, even if an exception happens. Shorter and safer.

## Custom exception for business rules

```java
public class InsufficientBalanceException extends RuntimeException {
    public InsufficientBalanceException(String accountNo, BigDecimal needed) {
        super("Account " + accountNo + " needs " + needed + " more");
    }
}

void withdraw(BigDecimal amount) {
    if (balance.compareTo(amount) < 0)
        throw new InsufficientBalanceException(accountNo, amount.subtract(balance));
    balance = balance.subtract(amount);
}
```
- Extending `RuntimeException` (unchecked) is the common choice in Spring apps. A `@RestControllerAdvice` can then turn it into a clean HTTP error.
- `super(message)` passes the message to the parent, so `getMessage()` works.
- `compareTo(amount) < 0` means balance < amount (BigDecimal can't use `<`).

**Tricky facts**
- Order catch blocks from **specific to general**. Putting `catch (Exception)` first makes the later ones unreachable, which is a compile error.
- If `finally` has a `return`, it **overrides** the return in `try`. Avoid it.
- `finally` does not run only if the JVM exits (`System.exit`) or crashes.
- `throw` throws one exception. `throws` in a method signature **declares** what might be thrown.

# Generics

**Generics** let a class or method work with **any type** while staying type-safe.

**Analogy:** a labelled storage box: "Box of Books". You can't accidentally put shoes in it, and when you take something out you *know* it's a book (no casting).

```java
// Without generics (old Java): unsafe
List list = new ArrayList();
list.add("hi"); list.add(42);
String s = (String) list.get(1);   // ClassCastException at runtime!

// With generics: the compiler stops mistakes
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("hi");
// names.add(42);                  // compile error
String first = names.get(0);       // no cast needed
```

## Writing a generic class and method

```java
public class Box<T> {                     // T = a type parameter
    private T value;
    public void set(T v) { value = v; }
    public T get() { return value; }
}
Box<Integer> b = new Box<>();             // T becomes Integer
b.set(5);

public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> list) {
    T best = list.get(0);
    for (T x : list) if (x.compareTo(best) > 0) best = x;
    return best;
}
max(List.of(3, 9, 2));      // 9
```

**Line by line**
- `<T>`: a placeholder. The user decides the real type: `Box<Integer>`, `Box<String>`.
- `<T extends Comparable<T>>`: a **bounded type**. T must be comparable, so we're allowed to call `compareTo`.
- `new Box<>()`: the diamond `<>` lets Java infer the type.

## Wildcards: PECS (Producer Extends, Consumer Super)

```java
double sum(List<? extends Number> nums) {      // READ numbers (producer)
    double s = 0; for (Number n : nums) s += n.doubleValue(); return s;
}
void addInts(List<? super Integer> out) {      // WRITE integers (consumer)
    out.add(1); out.add(2);
}
```
- `? extends Number`: accepts `List<Integer>`, `List<Double>`... You can **read** as Number, but you can't add (Java doesn't know the exact type).
- `? super Integer`: accepts `List<Integer>`, `List<Number>`, `List<Object>`. You can **add** Integers.

**Type erasure:** generics exist only at compile time. At runtime `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` are both just `List`. That's why you can't write `new T()` or `if (x instanceof List<String>)`.

# Interfaces vs abstract classes

| | Interface | Abstract class |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | A **capability / contract** ("can do") | A **partial base class** ("is a") |
| Multiple inheritance | A class can implement **many** | A class can extend only **one** |
| Fields | Only `public static final` constants | Any fields (with state) |
| Constructors | No | Yes |
| Methods | abstract, `default`, `static`, `private` (Java 9+) | abstract and concrete |

**Analogy:** an interface is a **driving licence**: it says you *can drive*, and anyone can get one. An abstract class is a **family surname**: you inherit traits, but you can only belong to one family.

```java
interface Payable {
    BigDecimal amountDue();                          // abstract (must implement)
    default boolean isOverdue(LocalDate due) {       // default: shared body
        return LocalDate.now().isAfter(due);
    }
}

abstract class Account {
    protected BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;  // state
    protected Account(BigDecimal opening) { balance = opening; }
    abstract BigDecimal monthlyFee();                // subclasses decide
    void applyFee() { balance = balance.subtract(monthlyFee()); }   // shared logic
}

class SavingsAccount extends Account implements Payable {
    SavingsAccount(BigDecimal opening) { super(opening); }
    BigDecimal monthlyFee() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    public BigDecimal amountDue() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `default` method: added in Java 8 so that interfaces can gain new methods **without breaking** existing implementations.
- `Account` has a field and a constructor, which an interface cannot have.
- `applyFee()` is a small **template method**: a fixed step that calls the variable step `monthlyFee()`.
- `SavingsAccount` extends **one** class but could implement **many** interfaces.

**Rule of thumb:** start with an interface. Use an abstract class when subclasses share **state or common code**.

# Enums and records

## Enum: a fixed set of named values

```java
public enum LoanStatus {
    PENDING("Waiting for approval"),
    ACTIVE("Disbursed and running"),
    CLOSED("Fully repaid");

    private final String label;
    LoanStatus(String label) { this.label = label; }
    public String label() { return label; }

    public boolean canRepay() { return this == ACTIVE; }
}

LoanStatus s = LoanStatus.valueOf("ACTIVE");   // String -> enum
switch (s) {
    case PENDING -> System.out.println("approve first");
    case ACTIVE  -> System.out.println("collect");
    case CLOSED  -> System.out.println("done");
}
```

**Line by line**
- Each constant (`PENDING`...) is a single object created once, so you can compare with `==`.
- Enums can have fields, constructors and methods, like a small class.
- The constructor is implicitly private: nobody can create new statuses at runtime.
- `valueOf("ACTIVE")` throws `IllegalArgumentException` for an unknown name.
- **Analogy:** traffic light colours. There are only three, and nobody can invent "purple".
- In JPA, store enums with `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)`, never ORDINAL: reordering the constants would silently corrupt the data.

## Record (Java 16+): an immutable data carrier

```java
public record MoneyDto(String currency, BigDecimal amount) {
    public MoneyDto {                                   // compact constructor: validation
        if (amount.signum() < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative");
    }
}
var m = new MoneyDto("BDT", new BigDecimal("500.00"));
m.amount();             // accessor (no "get" prefix)
System.out.println(m);  // MoneyDto[currency=BDT, amount=500.00]
```
- One line gives you: private final fields, a constructor, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode` and `toString`.
- The **compact constructor** runs your validation before the fields are assigned.
- Records are perfect for **DTOs**, API responses and events. They are not suitable as JPA entities (entities need a no-arg constructor and mutable fields).
- **Analogy:** a sealed envelope with a printed receipt. Once printed, the values can't change.

# Immutable class recipe

An **immutable** object can't change after creation (like `String`, `LocalDate`, `BigDecimal`). It is automatically thread-safe and safe to share or cache.

**Analogy:** a stamped, signed certificate. If anything needs to change, you issue a new certificate.

```java
public final class Installment {                          // 1. final class: no subclass can break it
    private final int number;                             // 2. private final fields
    private final BigDecimal amount;
    private final List<String> notes;

    public Installment(int number, BigDecimal amount, List<String> notes) {
        this.number = number;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.notes = List.copyOf(notes);                  // 3. defensive copy IN
    }

    public int getNumber() { return number; }             // 4. getters only, no setters
    public BigDecimal getAmount() { return amount; }
    public List<String> getNotes() { return notes; }      // List.copyOf is already unmodifiable

    public Installment withAmount(BigDecimal newAmount) { // 5. "change" returns a NEW object
        return new Installment(number, newAmount, notes);
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
1. `final class`: stops a subclass from adding mutable behaviour.
2. `private final`: fields are set once and hidden.
3. `List.copyOf(notes)`: if we stored the caller's list directly, the caller could still modify it from outside. The copy cuts that link. `List.copyOf` is also unmodifiable.
4. No setters.
5. `withAmount`: the "wither" pattern. You get a modified copy and the original stays unchanged.

`BigDecimal` and `String` fields are already immutable, so they need no copying. Mutable fields like `Date`, arrays and lists need defensive copies both in and out.

Shortcut: a `record` gives you points 1, 2 and 4. You still add `List.copyOf` in its compact constructor.

# LRU cache with LinkedHashMap

**LRU (Least Recently Used):** when the cache is full, throw out the item that hasn't been used for the longest time.

**Analogy:** a small desk that holds 3 books. When you need a 4th, you put back the book you haven't touched for the longest time.

```java
class LruCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;

    LruCache(int capacity) {
        super(16, 0.75f, true);            // true = ACCESS order (not insertion order)
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity;          // remove the oldest when over capacity
    }
}

LruCache<String, String> c = new LruCache<>(2);
c.put("a", "1"); c.put("b", "2");
c.get("a");             // 'a' is now most recently used
c.put("c", "3");        // over capacity: evicts 'b' (least recently used)
System.out.println(c.keySet());   // [a, c]
```

**Line by line**
- `LinkedHashMap` = HashMap + a doubly linked list that remembers order.
- `super(16, 0.75f, true)`: initial capacity, load factor, and **accessOrder = true**. Each `get`/`put` moves the entry to the end (most recent).
- `removeEldestEntry`: a hook that LinkedHashMap calls after every `put`. Returning true removes the first (least recently used) entry.
- Both `get` and `put` are O(1).

Interview follow-up: "Build it without LinkedHashMap." Use a `HashMap<K, Node>` plus your own doubly linked list: move a node to the head on access and remove from the tail when full.

# Lambdas and functional interfaces

A **lambda** is a short anonymous function: `(parameters) -> body`.
A **functional interface** has exactly **one abstract method**, and a lambda provides that method's body.

**Analogy:** a lambda is a sticky note with instructions ("double this number") that you hand to someone else to run when needed.

```java
// Old style: anonymous class
Comparator<String> c1 = new Comparator<String>() {
    public int compare(String a, String b) { return a.length() - b.length(); }
};
// Lambda: same thing
Comparator<String> c2 = (a, b) -> a.length() - b.length();
```

## The four core functional interfaces (java.util.function)

| Interface | Method | Takes → Returns | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| `Function<T,R>` | `apply` | T → R | `s -> s.length()` |
| `Predicate<T>` | `test` | T → boolean | `n -> n > 0` |
| `Supplier<T>` | `get` | nothing → T | `() -> LocalDate.now()` |
| `Consumer<T>` | `accept` | T → nothing | `s -> System.out.println(s)` |

```java
Function<String, Integer> len = s -> s.length();
Predicate<Integer> isPositive = n -> n > 0;
Supplier<List<String>> newList = ArrayList::new;
Consumer<String> printer = System.out::println;
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add = (a, b) -> a + b;
UnaryOperator<String> upper = String::toUpperCase;

len.apply("hello");              // 5
isPositive.test(-3);             // false
isPositive.and(n -> n < 100);    // combine predicates
len.andThen(n -> n * 2).apply("hi");   // 4

@FunctionalInterface
interface Validator { boolean validate(String input); }   // your own
Validator notBlank = s -> !s.isBlank();
```

**Line by line**
- `s -> s.length()`: one parameter, so no parentheses are needed. A single expression has an implicit `return`.
- `ArrayList::new`, `System.out::println`, `String::toUpperCase`: **method references**, a shorter way to write a lambda that just calls one method.
- `@FunctionalInterface`: optional, but the compiler then makes sure there's only one abstract method.

**Effectively final:** a lambda can use local variables from outside only if they never change after assignment.
```java
int base = 10;
Function<Integer, Integer> f = x -> x + base;   // OK
// base = 20;   // would make the lambda above a compile error
```

# synchronized, volatile and Atomic

## The problem: a race condition

```java
class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    void increment() { count++; }   // NOT atomic: read -> add 1 -> write
}
```
`count++` is really three steps. Two threads can both read 5, both write 6, and one increment is lost.
**Analogy:** two clerks update the same paper ledger. Both read "5", both write "6". One deposit disappears.

## Fix 1: synchronized (a lock)

```java
class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    synchronized void increment() { count++; }     // only one thread at a time
    synchronized int get() { return count; }
}
```
- `synchronized` on a method locks on `this`. Other threads calling any synchronized method of the **same object** wait.
- It also guarantees **visibility**: changes are flushed to main memory when the lock is released.
- **Analogy:** a single bathroom key. Whoever holds the key goes in; everyone else waits.

## Fix 2: AtomicInteger (lock-free)

```java
class Counter {
    private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();
    void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }  // atomic, uses CPU CAS
    int get() { return count.get(); }
}
```
- Uses **CAS (Compare-And-Swap)**: "set to 6 only if it is still 5, otherwise retry". There is no blocking, so it is fast for simple counters.

## volatile: visibility only, NOT atomicity

```java
class Worker implements Runnable {
    private volatile boolean running = true;   // every thread sees the latest value
    public void run() { while (running) { /* work */ } }
    public void stop() { running = false; }
}
```
- Without `volatile`, a thread may keep a cached copy of `running` and **never see** the change, so it loops forever.
- `volatile` makes every read go to main memory. It does **not** make `count++` safe.
- **Analogy:** a notice board everyone must check, instead of each person relying on their memory of yesterday's notice.

| Tool | Visibility | Atomicity | Use for |
|---|---|---|---|
| `volatile` | ✓ | ✗ | Flags (stop signals) |
| `AtomicInteger/Long` | ✓ | ✓ (single variable) | Counters |
| `synchronized` / `ReentrantLock` | ✓ | ✓ (whole block) | Several variables that change together |

`ReentrantLock` adds `tryLock(timeout)` (avoids waiting forever), fairness and multiple conditions. Always unlock in `finally`:
```java
lock.lock();
try { /* critical section */ } finally { lock.unlock(); }
```

# Producer–consumer and concurrent collections

**Producer–consumer:** one thread produces work, and another consumes it, through a shared queue.
**Analogy:** a bakery. The baker (producer) puts bread on a shelf (the queue), and the cashier (consumer) takes it. If the shelf is full the baker waits; if it is empty the cashier waits.

```java
BlockingQueue<String> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10);   // capacity 10

Thread producer = new Thread(() -> {
    try {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
            queue.put("payment-" + i);      // waits if the queue is full
        }
        queue.put("DONE");                  // poison pill: tells the consumer to stop
    } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
});

Thread consumer = new Thread(() -> {
    try {
        while (true) {
            String item = queue.take();     // waits if the queue is empty
            if (item.equals("DONE")) break;
            System.out.println("Processing " + item);
        }
    } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
});

producer.start(); consumer.start();
```

**Line by line**
- `ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10)`: a thread-safe queue with a fixed size.
- `put`: blocks when full, which gives **back-pressure** (the producer can't flood memory).
- `take`: blocks when empty. No busy-waiting loop is needed.
- `"DONE"` is a **poison pill**: a special message meaning "no more work".
- `Thread.currentThread().interrupt()`: restore the interrupt flag. Never silently swallow `InterruptedException`.

## ConcurrentHashMap
- A thread-safe Map without locking the whole map: it locks per bucket and uses CAS.
- Use atomic compound methods, not check-then-act:
```java
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> hits = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
hits.merge("home", 1, Integer::sum);          // atomic increment
// WRONG (race): if (!m.containsKey(k)) m.put(k, v);
hits.putIfAbsent("about", 0);                 // atomic
```
- Other thread-safe collections: `CopyOnWriteArrayList` (many reads, few writes), `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`.
- `Collections.synchronizedMap` locks the whole map, which is slower.

# Modern Java (17 / 21)

Spring Boot 3 requires Java 17+, so interviewers expect you to know these features.

```java
// 1. var (Java 10): the local type is inferred
var members = new ArrayList<Member>();     // type is ArrayList<Member>

// 2. Switch expressions (Java 14): return a value, no fall-through
String label = switch (status) {
    case PENDING -> "Waiting";
    case ACTIVE, OVERDUE -> "Running";
    case CLOSED -> {
        log.info("closed loan");
        yield "Done";                      // yield returns from a block
    }
};

// 3. Text blocks (Java 15): multi-line strings
String sql = """
        SELECT id, amount
        FROM loan
        WHERE status = 'ACTIVE'
        """;

// 4. Records (Java 16)
record Point(int x, int y) {}

// 5. Pattern matching for instanceof (Java 16)
if (obj instanceof String s && !s.isBlank()) {
    System.out.println(s.length());        // s is already cast
}

// 6. Sealed classes (Java 17): only listed subclasses are allowed
sealed interface Payment permits Cash, Bkash, BankTransfer {}
record Cash(BigDecimal amount) implements Payment {}
record Bkash(String phone, BigDecimal amount) implements Payment {}
record BankTransfer(String iban, BigDecimal amount) implements Payment {}

// 7. Pattern matching for switch + record patterns (Java 21)
String describe(Payment p) {
    return switch (p) {
        case Cash c -> "Cash " + c.amount();
        case Bkash(String phone, BigDecimal amt) -> "bKash from " + phone;
        case BankTransfer b when b.amount().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0 -> "Large transfer";
        case BankTransfer b -> "Transfer";
    };                                     // no default needed: the compiler knows all cases
}

// 8. Virtual threads (Java 21)
try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) exec.submit(() -> callSlowApi());
}
```

**Line by line**
1. `var`: less typing. It is still statically typed (the type can't change), and it only works for local variables.
2. A switch **expression** returns a value. `->` means no `break` is needed and there is no accidental fall-through. `yield` returns a value from a `{ }` block.
3. A text block keeps line breaks and removes common indentation. Great for SQL and JSON.
4. Records: see "Enums and Records".
5. `instanceof String s`: test and cast in one go. `s` is only in scope where the test is true.
6. `sealed ... permits`: a closed family of types. **Analogy:** a club with a fixed membership list.
7. The switch can **deconstruct** a record (`Bkash(String phone, ...)`) and add a `when` guard. Because `Payment` is sealed, the compiler checks that every case is covered.
8. Virtual threads are lightweight threads managed by the JVM. Blocking I/O no longer wastes an OS thread, so 10,000 concurrent calls are fine. In Spring Boot 3.2+: `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`.

# IoC and dependency injection

**IoC (Inversion of Control):** you don't create your dependencies with `new`. A **container** (Spring) creates them and hands them to you.
**DI (Dependency Injection):** the way IoC is done. Dependencies are "injected" through the constructor, a setter or a field.

**Analogy:** in a hotel you don't bring your own bed and towels; the hotel (the container) provides them. You just say what you need. You can swap a hard pillow for a soft one without changing yourself.

```java
// WITHOUT DI: tightly coupled
class LoanService {
    private final LoanRepository repo = new JdbcLoanRepository();   // stuck with this one
}

// WITH DI: loosely coupled
public interface LoanRepository { Loan save(Loan l); }

@Repository
class JpaLoanRepository implements LoanRepository { ... }

@Service
public class LoanService {
    private final LoanRepository repo;
    private final SmsSender sms;

    public LoanService(LoanRepository repo, SmsSender sms) {   // constructor injection
        this.repo = repo;
        this.sms = sms;
    }

    public Loan approve(Loan loan) {
        Loan saved = repo.save(loan);
        sms.send(loan.getPhone(), "Your loan is approved");
        return saved;
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `new JdbcLoanRepository()` hard-codes the implementation, so you can't swap it or mock it in tests.
- `@Repository` / `@Service`: tell Spring "create one object (a **bean**) of this class and manage it".
- Constructor parameters: Spring sees that LoanService needs a `LoanRepository` and an `SmsSender`, finds matching beans and passes them in.
- With a **single constructor**, `@Autowired` is optional.
- `private final`: dependencies can't change after construction (immutability).

## Three injection styles

| Style | Example | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **Constructor** | `LoanService(LoanRepository r)` | ✓ **Recommended**: final fields, easy to test, fails fast if missing |
| Setter | `@Autowired void setRepo(...)` | Optional dependencies only |
| Field | `@Autowired private LoanRepository repo;` | ✗ Hidden dependencies, can't be final, hard to test |

**Testing benefit:** `new LoanService(mockRepo, mockSms)` works in a plain unit test with no Spring at all.

## When two beans match
```java
@Service
public class NotificationService {
    public NotificationService(@Qualifier("smsSender") Sender sender) { ... }
}
// or mark one implementation @Primary
```
- With two `Sender` beans, Spring throws `NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException`. `@Qualifier` picks one by name, and `@Primary` sets the default.

**ApplicationContext** is the container that holds all the beans. `BeanFactory` is the basic version; ApplicationContext adds events, i18n and AOP.

# Bean scopes and lifecycle

## Scopes

| Scope | How many instances | Use |
|---|---|---|
| **singleton** (default) | One per container | Stateless services, repositories |
| prototype | A new one each time it is requested | Stateful helpers |
| request | One per HTTP request | Request-specific data |
| session | One per HTTP session | User session data |

**Analogy:** singleton = the office printer that everybody shares. Prototype = disposable paper cups, a new one each time.

**Warning:** singleton beans are shared by all threads (all users). **Never keep request-specific data in a field of a singleton.**

```java
@Service
public class BadService {
    private String currentUser;   // ✗ shared by ALL requests: user A can see user B's data
}
```

## Lifecycle

1. Instantiate (constructor)
2. Inject dependencies
3. `@PostConstruct` method runs
4. The bean is ready and used
5. On shutdown: `@PreDestroy` runs

```java
@Component
public class ExchangeRateCache {
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> rates = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final RateClient client;

    public ExchangeRateCache(RateClient client) { this.client = client; }

    @PostConstruct
    void load() {                                // runs once after injection
        rates.putAll(client.fetchAll());
    }

    @PreDestroy
    void cleanup() {                             // runs on graceful shutdown
        rates.clear();
    }
}

@Component
@Scope("prototype")                              // a NEW instance every time it is requested
class ReportBuilder { ... }
```

**Line by line**
- The constructor runs **before** injection is complete for field/setter injection, so put "start-up work that needs dependencies" in `@PostConstruct`.
- `@PostConstruct`: warm up caches, validate configuration.
- `@PreDestroy`: close connections, flush buffers.
- `ConcurrentHashMap`: because the singleton is shared across threads.
- `@Scope("prototype")` on `ReportBuilder`: each injection or `getBean()` call creates a fresh object, which is safe for holding per-report state.

**Prototype inside a singleton:** the prototype is injected only **once**, when the singleton is created. To get a fresh one each time, inject `ObjectProvider<MyProto>` and call `getObject()`.

**Circular dependency:** A needs B and B needs A through constructors. Spring Boot fails at startup. Fix the design (extract a third class) instead of hacking around it with `@Lazy`.

# Stereotypes and configuration

## Stereotype annotations (put on your own classes)

| Annotation | Layer | Extra behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| `@Component` | Generic bean | none |
| `@Service` | Business logic | none (documents intent) |
| `@Repository` | Data access | Translates DB exceptions into Spring's `DataAccessException` |
| `@Controller` | Web MVC (returns views) | Request mapping |
| `@RestController` | REST API | `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody` (returns JSON) |

They're all found by **component scanning**: Spring scans the package of your `@SpringBootApplication` class and its sub-packages.

## @Configuration + @Bean (for classes you didn't write)

```java
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public RestClient paymentClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        return builder.baseUrl("https://pay.example.com").build();
    }

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }

    @Bean
    @Profile("dev")                         // only in the dev profile
    public SmsSender fakeSms() { return new ConsoleSmsSender(); }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@Configuration`: "this class defines beans".
- `@Bean` method: its **return value** becomes a bean, and the method name (`paymentClient`) is the bean name.
- Parameters (like `RestClient.Builder`) are injected automatically.
- Use `@Bean` for **third-party classes** (you can't add `@Component` to library code) or when creation needs logic.
- `@Profile("dev")`: the bean exists only when the dev profile is active.

**Why `@Configuration` and not `@Component` for bean classes?** Spring proxies `@Configuration` classes so that calling one `@Bean` method from another returns the **same singleton** instead of creating a new object.

**Analogy:** `@Component` is a product that labels itself "please stock me". `@Bean` is the shop manager writing an order for a product made by another company.

# Spring AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming)

**AOP** adds the same behaviour (logging, security, transactions, timing) to many methods **without editing them**.

**Analogy:** airport security. Every passenger passes through the same checkpoint before boarding, but the planes and passengers don't contain any security code themselves.

Terms:
- **Aspect:** the class holding the cross-cutting logic.
- **Advice:** *what* to do and *when* (`@Before`, `@After`, `@AfterReturning`, `@AfterThrowing`, `@Around`).
- **Pointcut:** *where*: which methods (an expression).
- **Join point:** a point during execution (a method call) where the advice runs.

```java
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Timed {}                          // our own marker annotation

@Aspect
@Component
@Slf4j
public class TimingAspect {

    @Around("@annotation(Timed)")                   // pointcut: methods marked @Timed
    public Object time(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            return pjp.proceed();                   // run the real method
        } finally {
            log.info("{} took {} ms", pjp.getSignature().toShortString(),
                     System.currentTimeMillis() - start);
        }
    }
}

@Service
class ReportService {
    @Timed
    public Report buildParReport() { ... }          // automatically timed
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@interface Timed`: a custom annotation. `RUNTIME` retention is needed so that Spring can see it while the app runs.
- `@Aspect @Component`: registers the aspect as a bean.
- `@Around(...)`: wraps the method, so code runs **before and after**.
- `pjp.proceed()`: calls the original method. Forget it and the real method never runs.
- `finally`: logs the time even if the method throws.

## How it works: proxies
Spring wraps your bean in a **proxy** object. Callers talk to the proxy, which runs the advice and then calls your real method.

**Self-invocation trap:** if a method in the same class calls `this.buildParReport()`, the call **bypasses the proxy**, so no advice runs. The same applies to `@Transactional`, `@Cacheable` and `@Async`.

**Proxy types:** JDK dynamic proxy (when the bean implements an interface) or CGLIB (subclassing, the default in Boot). Because CGLIB subclasses, **final classes/methods and private methods can't be advised**.

# How Spring Boot works

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class LoanApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(LoanApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@SpringBootApplication` = three annotations in one:
  - `@SpringBootConfiguration`: this is a configuration class.
  - `@EnableAutoConfiguration`: configure beans automatically based on the classpath.
  - `@ComponentScan`: find `@Component`/`@Service`/... in this package and its sub-packages.
- `SpringApplication.run(...)`: creates the ApplicationContext, runs auto-configuration, starts the **embedded Tomcat** on port 8080.

## Starters
A **starter** is one dependency that pulls in a tested set of libraries.
- `spring-boot-starter-web`: Spring MVC + Jackson + Tomcat.
- `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa`: Hibernate + Spring Data + HikariCP.
- `spring-boot-starter-security`, `-validation`, `-actuator`, `-test`, `-data-redis`.

**Analogy:** a starter is a **meal kit**: one box with every ingredient for the recipe, in quantities that work together.

## Auto-configuration
Boot looks at what's on the classpath and creates beans for you **only if you haven't defined your own**.
- PostgreSQL driver + `spring.datasource.url` present → it creates a `DataSource` (HikariCP pool).
- Jackson present → it configures an `ObjectMapper`.

```java
// Inside Spring Boot (simplified) — how auto-config decides
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)            // only if the class exists on the classpath
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean                    // only if YOU didn't define one
    public DataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties props) { ... }
}
```
- `@ConditionalOnClass`: the library must be present.
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`: **your bean wins**. That's how you override defaults.
- See what was configured and why: run with `--debug` to get the "conditions evaluation report".
- The list of auto-configurations lives in `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`.

**Analogy:** a smart home. When it detects that you plugged in a TV, it sets up the remote automatically, unless you've already set one up yourself.

**Embedded server:** Tomcat runs inside your JAR. Deploy with just `java -jar app.jar`, no separate server install.

# Configuration and profiles

```yaml
# src/main/resources/application.yml
server:
  port: 8080
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/loans
    username: app
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}          # read from an environment variable
  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: validate              # never 'update' or 'create' in production
    open-in-view: false
loan:
  max-amount: 500000
  interest-rate: 0.12
  allowed-pos: [PO1, PO2]
```

```java
// Option 1: @Value for a single value
@Value("${loan.max-amount}")
private BigDecimal maxAmount;

// Option 2 (preferred): type-safe grouped properties
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "loan")
@Validated
public record LoanProperties(
        @NotNull BigDecimal maxAmount,
        @NotNull BigDecimal interestRate,
        List<String> allowedPos) {}

@SpringBootApplication
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan          // enables scanning for @ConfigurationProperties
public class LoanApplication { ... }

@Service
class LoanService {
    LoanService(LoanProperties props) { ... props.maxAmount() ... }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `${DB_PASSWORD}`: **never commit secrets**. Read them from environment variables or a secret store.
- `ddl-auto: validate`: Hibernate checks that the tables match the entities but never changes them. Use Flyway/Liquibase for schema changes.
- `open-in-view: false`: closes the DB session after the service layer (avoids hidden lazy queries in controllers).
- `@Value("${...}")`: fine for one or two values.
- `@ConfigurationProperties`: binds a whole group into an object. It is type-safe, validated with `@Validated`, and `max-amount` maps to `maxAmount` automatically (relaxed binding).

## Profiles: different settings per environment

```
application.yml          # shared defaults
application-dev.yml      # local DB, debug logs
application-prod.yml     # real DB, strict settings
```
Activate with `SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod` or `--spring.profiles.active=prod`.

**Analogy:** profiles are like clothes for different occasions. It's the same person (the app), dressed for home (dev), office (test) or a wedding (prod).

**Property precedence (highest wins):** command-line args → environment variables → `application-{profile}.yml` → `application.yml`. That's why Docker/K8s can override anything with environment variables (`SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL`).

# A REST controller for CRUD

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/members")
@RequiredArgsConstructor                     // Lombok: constructor for final fields
public class MemberController {

    private final MemberService service;

    @GetMapping
    public Page<MemberResponse> list(@RequestParam(required = false) String name,
                                     @PageableDefault(size = 20, sort = "name") Pageable pageable) {
        return service.search(name, pageable);
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public MemberResponse get(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return service.get(id);              // throws NotFoundException -> 404
    }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<MemberResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody MemberRequest req) {
        MemberResponse created = service.create(req);
        URI location = URI.create("/api/v1/members/" + created.id());
        return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(created);   // 201 + Location header
    }

    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public MemberResponse update(@PathVariable Long id, @Valid @RequestBody MemberRequest req) {
        return service.update(id, req);
    }

    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NO_CONTENT)   // 204
    public void delete(@PathVariable Long id) {
        service.delete(id);
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@RestController`: every method's return value is written as JSON (via Jackson).
- `@RequestMapping("/api/v1/members")`: the base URL. `v1` = API versioning.
- `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`: map the HTTP methods.
- `@PathVariable Long id`: takes `42` from `/members/42`.
- `@RequestParam`: reads `?name=rahim`. `required = false` makes it optional.
- `Pageable`: Spring builds it from `?page=0&size=20&sort=name,asc`. `Page<T>` returns the content plus totals.
- `@Valid @RequestBody`: turn the JSON body into a `MemberRequest`, **then validate it**. Invalid input → 400.
- `ResponseEntity.created(location)`: status **201 Created** plus a `Location` header pointing to the new resource.
- `@ResponseStatus(NO_CONTENT)`: a successful delete with no body → **204**.

**Analogy:** the controller is a receptionist who checks your form is filled in properly, sends it to the right department, and hands you back a receipt.

# DTOs and validation

A **DTO (Data Transfer Object)** defines exactly what the API accepts or returns, separate from the database entity.
**Analogy:** a bank deposit slip. It asks only for what's needed. You don't hand the clerk your whole bank file.

```java
public record MemberRequest(
        @NotBlank @Size(max = 100)           String name,
        @NotBlank @Pattern(regexp = "\\d{10}|\\d{13}|\\d{17}",
                           message = "NID must be 10, 13 or 17 digits")
                                             String nationalId,
        @NotBlank @Pattern(regexp = "01[3-9]\\d{8}", message = "Invalid BD mobile")
                                             String phone,
        @NotNull @Past                       LocalDate dateOfBirth,
        @NotNull @Positive                   Long samityId) {}

public record LoanRequest(
        @NotNull Long memberId,
        @NotNull @DecimalMin("1000.00") @DecimalMax("500000.00")
        @Digits(integer = 12, fraction = 2)  BigDecimal amount,
        @Min(1) @Max(60)                     int months) {}

public record MemberResponse(Long id, String name, String phone, String samityName) {
    public static MemberResponse from(Member m) {
        return new MemberResponse(m.getId(), m.getName(), m.getPhone(), m.getSamity().getName());
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@NotBlank`: not null and not just spaces (for Strings). `@NotNull`: just not null (for any type).
- `@Size(max = 100)`: limits string length (match it to the DB column size).
- `@Pattern(regexp = ...)`: must match the regex. `message` customises the error text.
- `@Past`: the date must be before today.
- `@DecimalMin/@DecimalMax`: a range for BigDecimal. `@Digits`: maximum integer and fraction digits.
- `@Min/@Max`: a range for whole numbers.
- `MemberResponse.from(entity)`: a small **mapper** that converts entity → DTO and exposes only safe fields. MapStruct can generate mappers for big projects.
- Validation only runs when the parameter is marked `@Valid` (or the class is `@Validated`).

## Custom validator (business rule)

```java
@Target(ElementType.FIELD) @Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = BdPhoneValidator.class)
public @interface BdPhone {
    String message() default "Invalid Bangladeshi phone";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}

public class BdPhoneValidator implements ConstraintValidator<BdPhone, String> {
    public boolean isValid(String v, ConstraintValidatorContext ctx) {
        return v == null || v.matches("01[3-9]\\d{8}");   // null is handled by @NotNull
    }
}
```
- `@Constraint(validatedBy = ...)`: links the annotation to the class that checks it.
- `message`, `groups` and `payload` are required boilerplate for every constraint annotation.
- Returning `true` for null is the convention: let `@NotNull` decide whether null is allowed.

# Global exception handling

Instead of try/catch in every controller, handle errors **in one place** and return a consistent JSON error.

**Analogy:** a hospital's emergency desk. Whatever goes wrong anywhere in the building, patients are routed to one desk that follows a standard procedure.

```java
public class NotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public NotFoundException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}
public class BusinessRuleException extends RuntimeException {
    public BusinessRuleException(String msg) { super(msg); }
}

@RestControllerAdvice
@Slf4j
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(NotFoundException.class)
    public ProblemDetail notFound(NotFoundException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(BusinessRuleException.class)
    public ProblemDetail rule(BusinessRuleException ex) {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, ex.getMessage());
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ProblemDetail validation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
        pd.setTitle("Validation failed");
        Map<String, String> errors = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()
          .forEach(fe -> errors.put(fe.getField(), fe.getDefaultMessage()));
        pd.setProperty("errors", errors);
        return pd;
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException.class)
    public ProblemDetail conflict() {
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.CONFLICT,
                "Record was changed by someone else. Reload and try again.");
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ProblemDetail unexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("Unexpected error", ex);                     // full details in the LOG
        return ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR,
                "Something went wrong");                        // generic message to the CLIENT
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@RestControllerAdvice`: applies to **all** controllers and returns JSON.
- `@ExceptionHandler(X.class)`: "when X is thrown anywhere, run this method".
- `ProblemDetail` (Spring 6): the standard **RFC 7807** error format: `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`.
- `MethodArgumentNotValidException`: thrown when `@Valid` fails. We collect each field's error into a map.
- `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`: two users edited the same record, so return **409 Conflict**.
- Catch-all `Exception`: log the details, but **never send the stack trace to the client** (security).
- Spring picks the **most specific** handler first.

Example response:
```json
{ "type": "about:blank", "title": "Validation failed", "status": 400,
  "errors": { "phone": "Invalid BD mobile", "name": "must not be blank" } }
```

# OpenAPI, file upload and HTTP clients

## OpenAPI / Swagger: auto-generated API docs

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
  <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
  <version>2.6.0</version>
</dependency>
```
```java
@Operation(summary = "Create a member", description = "Registers a new member in a samity")
@ApiResponse(responseCode = "201", description = "Created")
@PostMapping
public ResponseEntity<MemberResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody MemberRequest req) { ... }
```
- Open `/swagger-ui.html` to get interactive docs where you can try requests. The raw spec is at `/v3/api-docs`.
- **Analogy:** a restaurant menu with photos. Clients know exactly what they can order and what arrives.

## File upload

```java
@PostMapping(value = "/members/{id}/photo", consumes = MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE)
public void uploadPhoto(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file) throws IOException {
    if (file.isEmpty()) throw new BusinessRuleException("Empty file");
    if (!List.of("image/jpeg", "image/png").contains(file.getContentType()))
        throw new BusinessRuleException("Only JPG/PNG allowed");
    Path target = Path.of("uploads", id + ".jpg");
    Files.copy(file.getInputStream(), target, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
}
```
- `consumes = MULTIPART_FORM_DATA`: the request is a form upload, not JSON.
- **Validate the type and size.** Never trust the original file name (path traversal: `../../etc/passwd`). Generate your own name.
- Size limits: `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=5MB`.

## Calling other APIs: RestClient (Spring 6.1+)

```java
@Service
public class PaymentGatewayClient {
    private final RestClient client;

    public PaymentGatewayClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        this.client = builder.baseUrl("https://gateway.example.com").build();
    }

    public PaymentResult charge(PaymentRequest req) {
        return client.post()
                .uri("/payments")
                .header("Idempotency-Key", req.reference())
                .body(req)
                .retrieve()
                .onStatus(HttpStatusCode::is4xxClientError,
                          (rq, rs) -> { throw new BusinessRuleException("Payment rejected"); })
                .body(PaymentResult.class);
    }
}
```
- `RestClient`: a modern, fluent, **synchronous** client (it replaces the old `RestTemplate`).
- `.body(req)` serialises the object to JSON. `.body(PaymentResult.class)` parses the response.
- `onStatus`: turn HTTP errors into your own exceptions.
- `Idempotency-Key`: lets the gateway detect a retried request so that it doesn't charge twice.
- `WebClient`: the reactive/non-blocking alternative. **OpenFeign**: declarative clients (an interface + annotations) used in microservices.
- **Always set timeouts** (connect/read), or a slow remote API can hang all your threads.

# Entity mapping

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "loan")
@Getter @Setter
@NoArgsConstructor(access = AccessLevel.PROTECTED)
public class Loan {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true, length = 30)
    private String loanNo;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 14, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal principal;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    @Column(nullable = false, length = 20)
    private LoanStatus status = LoanStatus.PENDING;

    private LocalDate disbursedOn;

    @Version
    private Long version;                          // optimistic locking

    @CreationTimestamp
    private Instant createdAt;

    @Transient
    private BigDecimal displayOnly;                // not stored in the DB
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@Entity`: this class maps to a table. `@Table(name = "loan")`: the exact table name.
- JPA **needs a no-arg constructor** (Hibernate creates objects by reflection). `PROTECTED` hides it from normal code.
- `@Id`: the primary key.
- `GenerationType.IDENTITY`: the DB auto-increments (`BIGSERIAL`). `SEQUENCE` uses a DB sequence and is better for batch inserts in Postgres.
- `@Column(nullable=false, unique=true, length=30)`: documents the constraints (the real ones should be in your migration SQL).
- `precision = 14, scale = 2`: `NUMERIC(14,2)` for money.
- `@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)`: stores `"ACTIVE"`, not `1`. ORDINAL breaks if you reorder the enum.
- `@Version`: Hibernate increments it on each update and rejects stale updates (see Locking).
- `@CreationTimestamp`: filled automatically on insert.
- `@Transient`: a field that is not persisted.

**Entity equality tip:** don't use Lombok `@Data` on entities. Its generated `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString` touch lazy relations, which causes extra queries or infinite loops.

## Spring Data auditing (who and when)

```java
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @CreatedDate     private Instant createdAt;
    @LastModifiedDate private Instant updatedAt;
    @CreatedBy       private String createdBy;
    @LastModifiedBy  private String updatedBy;
}
// + @EnableJpaAuditing and an AuditorAware<String> bean that returns the logged-in username
```
- `@MappedSuperclass`: its fields are copied into each subclass's table, so there is no separate table.
- Every entity that `extends Auditable` gets automatic audit columns, which is important for financial data.

# Relationships

```java
@Entity
public class Member {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) private Long id;
    private String name;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
    @JoinColumn(name = "samity_id")                       // FK column in the member table
    private Samity samity;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "member", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Loan> loans = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addLoan(Loan loan) {                      // helper keeps BOTH sides in sync
        loans.add(loan);
        loan.setMember(this);
    }
}

@Entity
public class Loan {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) private Long id;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "member_id", nullable = false)
    private Member member;                                // OWNING side (has the FK)
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@ManyToOne`: many members belong to one samity. The **FK lives on this side** (`samity_id`).
- `fetch = LAZY`: don't load the Samity until it's accessed. **The default for @ManyToOne is EAGER, so always set LAZY.**
- `@JoinColumn(name = "member_id")`: the FK column name.
- `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "member")`: the **inverse side**. "The FK is managed by the `member` field in Loan." It has no column of its own.
- **Owning side** = the side with the FK (the `@ManyToOne`). Only changes on the owning side are saved. That's why `addLoan` sets **both** sides.
- `cascade = ALL`: saving or deleting a member also saves or deletes its loans.
- `orphanRemoval = true`: removing a loan from the list deletes it from the DB.

**Analogy:** in a school, each student's form has a "class teacher" field (the owning side, the FK). The teacher's list of students is just a view built from those forms.

| Annotation | Default fetch |
|---|---|
| `@ManyToOne`, `@OneToOne` | **EAGER** (change it to LAZY!) |
| `@OneToMany`, `@ManyToMany` | LAZY |

## Many-to-many
```java
@ManyToMany
@JoinTable(name = "user_role",
           joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "user_id"),
           inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "role_id"))
private Set<Role> roles = new HashSet<>();
```
- This creates a join table. If the link needs extra columns (e.g. `assigned_at`), model the join table as its own entity with two `@ManyToOne` fields instead.

**Careful with cascade REMOVE on financial data:** you usually never delete loans. Use a status (`CLOSED`) or soft delete.

# Spring Data repositories

You write an **interface**, and Spring generates the implementation at runtime.

```java
public interface LoanRepository extends JpaRepository<Loan, Long> {

    // 1. Derived queries: SQL is generated from the method name
    List<Loan> findByStatus(LoanStatus status);
    Optional<Loan> findByLoanNo(String loanNo);
    List<Loan> findByMemberIdAndStatusOrderByDisbursedOnDesc(Long memberId, LoanStatus status);
    boolean existsByLoanNo(String loanNo);
    long countByStatus(LoanStatus status);
    Page<Loan> findByPrincipalGreaterThan(BigDecimal min, Pageable pageable);

    // 2. JPQL: queries on ENTITIES and fields, not tables
    @Query("select l from Loan l where l.member.samity.id = :samityId and l.status = 'ACTIVE'")
    List<Loan> activeInSamity(@Param("samityId") Long samityId);

    // 3. Native SQL for Postgres-specific features
    @Query(value = """
            SELECT po_id, SUM(outstanding) AS total
            FROM loan WHERE status = 'ACTIVE' GROUP BY po_id
            """, nativeQuery = true)
    List<PoOutstanding> outstandingPerPo();

    // 4. Update query
    @Modifying
    @Query("update Loan l set l.status = 'OVERDUE' where l.nextDueDate < :today and l.status = 'ACTIVE'")
    int markOverdue(@Param("today") LocalDate today);
}

public interface PoOutstanding {          // interface projection: only these columns
    Long getPoId();
    BigDecimal getTotal();
}
```

**Line by line**
- `JpaRepository<Loan, Long>`: entity type and ID type. You get `save`, `findById`, `findAll(Pageable)`, `deleteById`, `count`... for free.
- `findByStatus`: Spring parses the name: find... By... `Status` → `WHERE status = ?`.
- `And`, `Or`, `OrderBy...Desc`, `GreaterThan`, `Between`, `Like`, `In`, `IsNull`: keywords in method names.
- `member.samity.id` in JPQL: navigates relationships. Hibernate writes the joins.
- `:samityId` + `@Param`: a **named parameter**. Values are bound safely (no SQL injection).
- `nativeQuery = true`: raw SQL. It is DB-specific but lets you use window functions, CTEs, etc.
- `@Modifying`: required for UPDATE/DELETE queries. It needs a transaction. Returns the number of rows changed.
- **Projection interface** (`PoOutstanding`): reads only the needed columns, which is faster than loading full entities.

**Analogy:** a derived query is like telling a librarian "find books by author Rahman ordered by year". The sentence itself is the query.

## Dynamic filters: Specifications
```java
public interface LoanRepository extends JpaRepository<Loan, Long>, JpaSpecificationExecutor<Loan> {}

static Specification<Loan> hasStatus(LoanStatus s) {
    return (root, query, cb) -> s == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("status"), s);
}
static Specification<Loan> minAmount(BigDecimal min) {
    return (root, query, cb) -> min == null ? null : cb.ge(root.get("principal"), min);
}
repo.findAll(hasStatus(status).and(minAmount(min)), pageable);
```
- Each Specification is one optional WHERE condition. Returning `null` means "no filter". Combine them with `and`/`or` for search screens with many optional filters.

# The N+1 problem

You load **N** parents with 1 query, then touching each parent's lazy relation fires **1 more query per parent**: **N+1 queries** in total.

**Analogy:** you need the phone numbers of 100 students. Instead of asking the office once for the full list, you walk to the office 100 times, once per student.

```java
// Service code that looks innocent
List<Loan> loans = loanRepo.findAll();                 // 1 query
for (Loan l : loans) {
    System.out.println(l.getMember().getName());       // +1 query PER loan (lazy load)
}
// 100 loans -> 101 queries!
```

## Fixes

```java
public interface LoanRepository extends JpaRepository<Loan, Long> {

    // Fix 1: JOIN FETCH loads loans + members in ONE query
    @Query("select l from Loan l join fetch l.member where l.status = :status")
    List<Loan> findWithMember(@Param("status") LoanStatus status);

    // Fix 2: @EntityGraph does the same declaratively
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"member", "member.samity"})
    List<Loan> findByStatus(LoanStatus status);

    // Fix 3: a DTO projection selects only what's needed (best for read-only screens)
    @Query("select new bd.org.pksf.loan.LoanRow(l.id, l.loanNo, m.name, l.principal) " +
           "from Loan l join l.member m where l.status = :status")
    List<LoanRow> rows(@Param("status") LoanStatus status);
}
```
```yaml
# Fix 4: batch fetching loads lazy relations in groups of 50 with IN (...)
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.default_batch_fetch_size: 50
```

**Line by line**
- `join fetch l.member`: a SQL JOIN **and** fills in the `member` field, so there is no extra query later.
- `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = ...)`: "also load these relations eagerly, for this query only".
- `select new ...LoanRow(...)`: builds DTOs directly from the query. Nothing is managed by Hibernate, so it is the fastest option for lists and reports.
- `default_batch_fetch_size`: if you still lazy-load, Hibernate loads 50 members with one `WHERE id IN (...)` instead of 50 single queries.

**Careful:** `join fetch` of a **collection** combined with pagination makes Hibernate paginate in memory (warning HHH90003004). For pages, fetch the parent IDs first, or use batch fetching.

**How to detect N+1:** turn on SQL logging (`spring.jpa.show-sql=true` or `logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG`) and count the queries, or use Hibernate statistics.

**LazyInitializationException:** you touched a lazy relation **after the transaction/session closed** (e.g. in the controller with `open-in-view: false`). Fix it by fetching what you need in the service (JOIN FETCH/DTO), not by switching to EAGER.

# Transactions with @Transactional

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class RepaymentService {

    private final LoanRepository loans;
    private final TransactionRepository txns;
    private final AuditService audit;

    @Transactional                                   // all or nothing
    public void repay(Long loanId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Loan loan = loans.findById(loanId).orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException("Loan"));
        if (amount.compareTo(loan.getOutstanding()) > 0)
            throw new BusinessRuleException("Amount exceeds outstanding");

        loan.setOutstanding(loan.getOutstanding().subtract(amount));   // no save() needed: dirty checking
        txns.save(new LedgerEntry(loan, amount, "REPAYMENT"));
        audit.log("REPAY", loanId);                  // runs in its own transaction (REQUIRES_NEW)
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)                  // optimisation for reads
    public LoanResponse get(Long id) { ... }
}

@Service
class AuditService {
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void log(String action, Long id) { ... } // saved even if the caller later rolls back
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@Transactional`: Spring's proxy starts a transaction before the method and **commits** after it. If a `RuntimeException` escapes, it **rolls back**.
- Updating the outstanding and inserting the ledger entry happen together or not at all. **Analogy:** a cashier only gives the receipt once the money is actually in the drawer. Either both happen or neither.
- `loan.setOutstanding(...)` with no `save()`: the loan is **managed**, so Hibernate notices the change (**dirty checking**) and issues an UPDATE on commit.
- `readOnly = true`: Hibernate skips dirty checking, and the DB may optimise. Use it for queries.
- `REQUIRES_NEW`: suspends the outer transaction and runs in a new one. The audit row stays even if the repayment fails.

## Propagation (what happens if a transaction already exists?)

| Propagation | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| **REQUIRED** (default) | Join the existing one, or create a new one |
| REQUIRES_NEW | Always a new one (suspend the outer) |
| SUPPORTS | Join if one exists, otherwise run without |
| MANDATORY | Must already exist, otherwise throw |
| NOT_SUPPORTED | Run without a transaction (suspend the outer) |
| NEVER | Throw if one exists |
| NESTED | A savepoint inside the outer transaction |

## Rollback rules (a classic trap)
- By default it rolls back on **unchecked** exceptions (RuntimeException, Error) only.
- **Checked exceptions do NOT roll back.** Use `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)` if needed.
- If you catch the exception inside the method and don't rethrow it, the transaction **commits**.

## Why @Transactional sometimes "doesn't work"
1. **Self-invocation:** `this.repay()` from the same class skips the proxy.
2. The method is **private** (proxies only intercept public methods).
3. The exception is checked or swallowed.
4. The class isn't a Spring bean (created with `new`).

**Isolation:** `@Transactional(isolation = Isolation.SERIALIZABLE)`. The default uses the DB default (Read Committed in Postgres).

# Locking: preventing lost updates

**Problem:** two officers open the same loan (outstanding 5,000). Both record 1,000 repayments. Both save 4,000. **One payment is lost.**

## Optimistic locking: "assume no conflict, check at the end"

```java
@Entity
public class Loan {
    @Version
    private Long version;
    ...
}
```
What Hibernate runs:
```sql
UPDATE loan SET outstanding = 4000, version = 6
WHERE id = 42 AND version = 5;      -- 0 rows updated if someone else already changed it
```
- If 0 rows are updated, Hibernate throws `ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`, which we map to **409 Conflict** ("reload and try again").
- No DB locks are held, so it scales well. Best when conflicts are **rare**.
- **Analogy:** editing a shared Google Doc offline. When you sync, if someone else changed it first, you're asked to merge.

## Pessimistic locking: "lock first, then work"

```java
public interface LoanRepository extends JpaRepository<Loan, Long> {
    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
    @Query("select l from Loan l where l.id = :id")
    Optional<Loan> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
}

@Transactional
public void repay(Long id, BigDecimal amt) {
    Loan loan = repo.findByIdForUpdate(id).orElseThrow();   // SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
    loan.setOutstanding(loan.getOutstanding().subtract(amt));
}                                                            // lock released on commit
```
- `PESSIMISTIC_WRITE` → SQL `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`: other transactions trying to lock that row **wait**.
- It must run inside a transaction, because the lock is held until commit/rollback.
- Best when conflicts are **frequent** or the cost of a conflict is high (account balances).
- Risk: waiting and deadlocks. Keep the transactions short. You can set a lock timeout hint.
- **Analogy:** taking the only key to the record room. Others wait outside until you return it.

| | Optimistic | Pessimistic |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Version column check | DB row lock |
| Conflicts | Detected at commit → retry | Prevented by waiting |
| Best for | Low contention, web forms | High contention, balances |

# Other Spring capabilities

```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableScheduling
@EnableAsync
@EnableCaching
public class LoanApplication { ... }

@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
class OverdueJob {
    private final LoanRepository repo;

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 1 * * *", zone = "Asia/Dhaka")    // every day at 01:00
    @Transactional
    public void markOverdue() {
        int n = repo.markOverdue(LocalDate.now());
        log.info("Marked {} loans overdue", n);
    }
}

@Service
class NotificationService {
    @Async                                                   // runs on another thread
    public CompletableFuture<Void> sendSms(String phone, String text) {
        smsGateway.send(phone, text);
        return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(null);
    }
}

@Service
class PoService {
    @Cacheable(value = "po", key = "#id")                    // cache the result by id
    public PoDto get(Long id) { return mapper.toDto(repo.findById(id).orElseThrow()); }

    @CacheEvict(value = "po", key = "#id")                   // remove from the cache on update
    public void update(Long id, PoRequest req) { ... }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 1 * * *")`: the fields are **second minute hour day month weekday**. This means 01:00:00 every day. `fixedRate = 60000` means every 60 seconds.
- `zone = "Asia/Dhaka"`: without it the server's time zone is used.
- With **several app instances**, every instance runs the job. Use **ShedLock** (a DB lock) so that only one does.
- `@Async`: the caller returns immediately and the method runs in a thread pool. Configure the pool (`ThreadPoolTaskExecutor`), otherwise the defaults may not suit you.
- `@Cacheable`: the first call runs the method and stores the result. Later calls with the same key return the cached value without running the method.
- `@CacheEvict`: delete the stale entry when data changes. The backing store can be Redis (see Phase 7).
- All of these use **proxies**, so self-invocation won't trigger them.

**Spring events:** `ApplicationEventPublisher.publishEvent(...)` + `@EventListener`. `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` runs only if the transaction committed, which is perfect for "send SMS after the payment is saved".

**Spring Batch:** for large jobs such as "import 1 million installments from CSV every night". It uses chunks (read → process → write 1000 at a time) and supports restart from failure.

# MongoDB basics

| SQL | MongoDB |
|---|---|
| Database | Database |
| Table | Collection |
| Row | Document (BSON: binary JSON) |
| Column | Field |
| JOIN | Embedding or `$lookup` |
| Primary key | `_id` (ObjectId by default) |

**Analogy:** SQL is an Excel sheet with fixed columns. MongoDB is a folder of filled-in forms, where each form can have slightly different sections.

```javascript
// Insert
db.surveys.insertOne({
  memberId: 42,
  poCode: "PO-07",
  date: ISODate("2025-11-01"),
  answers: { income: 12000, householdSize: 5 },
  tags: ["rural", "agri"]
});

// Find: members with income > 10000, only some fields, newest first
db.surveys.find(
  { "answers.income": { $gt: 10000 }, tags: "agri" },
  { memberId: 1, "answers.income": 1, _id: 0 }
).sort({ date: -1 }).limit(10);

// Update
db.surveys.updateOne({ memberId: 42 }, { $set: { "answers.income": 15000 }, $push: { tags: "verified" } });

// Index
db.surveys.createIndex({ poCode: 1, date: -1 });
```

**Line by line**
- `insertOne`: no table definition is needed first. The document **is** the schema.
- `"answers.income"`: dot notation reaches into nested fields.
- `$gt`: greater than (others: `$lt`, `$in`, `$ne`, `$exists`).
- `tags: "agri"`: matches if the array **contains** "agri".
- The second argument of `find` is a **projection**: 1 = include, 0 = exclude.
- `$set` changes fields, `$push` appends to an array.
- `createIndex({poCode: 1, date: -1})`: a compound index (1 = ascending, -1 = descending).

## Spring Data MongoDB
```java
@Document("surveys")
public record Survey(@Id String id, Long memberId, String poCode, LocalDate date, Map<String, Object> answers) {}

public interface SurveyRepository extends MongoRepository<Survey, String> {
    List<Survey> findByPoCodeOrderByDateDesc(String poCode);
}
```
- It works just like JPA repositories: derived queries from method names.

# XML with JAXB and Jackson XML

Banks and government systems often still use **XML**. Spring can produce JSON **or** XML from the same endpoint.

```xml
<!-- dependency -->
<dependency>
  <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat</groupId>
  <artifactId>jackson-dataformat-xml</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```java
@JacksonXmlRootElement(localName = "Loan")
public record LoanXml(
        @JacksonXmlProperty(isAttribute = true) Long id,
        @JacksonXmlProperty(localName = "MemberName") String memberName,
        @JacksonXmlProperty(localName = "Amount") BigDecimal amount) {}

@GetMapping(value = "/loans/{id}", produces = { MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE, MediaType.APPLICATION_XML_VALUE })
public LoanXml get(@PathVariable Long id) { ... }
```
Request with `Accept: application/xml` → response:
```xml
<Loan id="7">
  <MemberName>Rahim</MemberName>
  <Amount>50000.00</Amount>
</Loan>
```

**Line by line**
- With `jackson-dataformat-xml` on the classpath, Spring can write XML.
- `@JacksonXmlRootElement(localName = "Loan")`: the outer tag name.
- `isAttribute = true`: `id` becomes `<Loan id="7">` instead of a child element.
- `produces = {JSON, XML}`: **content negotiation**. The client's `Accept` header decides the format.

## JAXB (Jakarta XML Binding): the classic standard

```java
@XmlRootElement(name = "Member")
@XmlAccessorType(XmlAccessType.FIELD)
public class MemberXml {
    @XmlAttribute private Long id;
    @XmlElement(name = "Name") private String name;
}

JAXBContext ctx = JAXBContext.newInstance(MemberXml.class);
Marshaller m = ctx.createMarshaller();
m.setProperty(Marshaller.JAXB_FORMATTED_OUTPUT, true);
m.marshal(member, System.out);                                       // object -> XML
MemberXml back = (MemberXml) ctx.createUnmarshaller().unmarshal(new File("member.xml"));  // XML -> object
```
- **Marshal** = object → XML. **Unmarshal** = XML → object.
- `@XmlAccessorType(FIELD)`: map fields directly (no getters needed).
- JAXB is used heavily with SOAP and **XSD** schemas (XSD = the rulebook describing which XML is valid).

**Security:** disable external entities when parsing untrusted XML, to prevent **XXE attacks** (malicious XML that reads server files).

**JSON vs XML:** JSON is lighter and native to JavaScript. XML supports attributes, namespaces and strict schema validation (XSD), which is why older enterprise systems use it.

# SOAP web services

**SOAP** is a strict **XML-based protocol** with a formal contract (the **WSDL**). It is common in banking, telecom and government integrations.

**Analogy:** REST is a casual phone call. SOAP is a formal government letter with a fixed format, an official envelope, stamps and a reference number.

## The SOAP message
```xml
<soapenv:Envelope xmlns:soapenv="http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/soap/envelope/"
                  xmlns:ln="http://pksf.org.bd/loans">
  <soapenv:Header/>                        <!-- optional: security tokens, transaction ids -->
  <soapenv:Body>
    <ln:GetLoanRequest>
      <ln:loanId>7</ln:loanId>
    </ln:GetLoanRequest>
  </soapenv:Body>
</soapenv:Envelope>
```
- **Envelope:** the outer wrapper. **Header:** metadata (WS-Security). **Body:** the actual request. **Fault:** an error response.
- It is always sent as HTTP **POST** to one URL. The operation is named inside the body.

## WSDL: the contract
An XML document describing the **operations** (GetLoan), the **messages** (GetLoanRequest/Response), the **types** (XSD) and the **endpoint address**. Tools generate client code from it.

## Contract-first SOAP endpoint with Spring-WS

```java
@Endpoint
public class LoanEndpoint {
    private static final String NS = "http://pksf.org.bd/loans";
    private final LoanService service;
    public LoanEndpoint(LoanService service) { this.service = service; }

    @PayloadRoot(namespace = NS, localPart = "GetLoanRequest")
    @ResponsePayload
    public GetLoanResponse getLoan(@RequestPayload GetLoanRequest request) {
        GetLoanResponse resp = new GetLoanResponse();
        resp.setLoan(service.findSoapLoan(request.getLoanId()));
        return resp;
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@Endpoint`: like `@RestController`, but for SOAP.
- `@PayloadRoot(namespace, localPart)`: routes messages whose body root element is `<GetLoanRequest>` in that namespace to this method.
- `@RequestPayload` / `@ResponsePayload`: Spring unmarshals the XML into a JAXB object and marshals the response back.
- `GetLoanRequest`/`GetLoanResponse` are **generated from the XSD** (contract-first) with the `jaxb2-maven-plugin`.
- A `WsConfigurerAdapter` config class exposes the WSDL at `/ws/loans.wsdl`.

## Calling a SOAP service (client)
```java
public class BankClient extends WebServiceGatewaySupport {
    public BalanceResponse balance(String acc) {
        BalanceRequest req = new BalanceRequest();
        req.setAccountNo(acc);
        return (BalanceResponse) getWebServiceTemplate().marshalSendAndReceive(req);
    }
}
```
- `marshalSendAndReceive`: object → SOAP XML → HTTP POST → parse the response XML → object.

| | REST | SOAP |
|---|---|---|
| Format | JSON (usually), any | XML only |
| Contract | OpenAPI (optional) | WSDL (mandatory) |
| Transport | HTTP verbs | Usually POST over HTTP |
| Standards | Simple | WS-Security, WS-ReliableMessaging, ACID transactions |
| Best for | Web/mobile apps | Strict enterprise/bank integrations |

# Service communication

## Synchronous (request → wait → response)
REST/gRPC calls. Simple, but the caller **waits** and **fails if the callee is down**.

```java
@FeignClient(name = "member-service")              // resolved via discovery / K8s DNS
public interface MemberClient {
    @GetMapping("/members/{id}")
    MemberDto get(@PathVariable Long id);
}

@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
class LoanService {
    private final MemberClient members;
    LoanDto create(LoanRequest req) {
        MemberDto m = members.get(req.memberId());  // a remote HTTP call that looks like a method call
        if (!m.active()) throw new BusinessRuleException("Member inactive");
        ...
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@FeignClient`: you write only an interface, and Spring Cloud OpenFeign generates the HTTP client.
- `name = "member-service"`: the logical service name, not a URL.
- It looks like a local call, but **it's the network**: it can be slow or fail. Add timeouts, retries and a circuit breaker.
- Enable it with `@EnableFeignClients`.

## Asynchronous (fire an event, don't wait)

```java
// loan-service publishes
kafkaTemplate.send("loan-approved", loan.getId().toString(), new LoanApproved(loan.getId(), loan.getMemberPhone()));

// notification-service consumes, whenever it's ready
@KafkaListener(topics = "loan-approved", groupId = "notification")
void on(LoanApproved e) { sms.send(e.phone(), "Your loan is approved"); }
```
- loan-service doesn't know or care who listens. If notification-service is down, the messages wait in Kafka until it comes back.

| | Sync (REST/Feign) | Async (Kafka/RabbitMQ) |
|---|---|---|
| Coupling | Temporal: both must be up | Loose |
| Response | Immediate | Eventually |
| Use for | Queries that need an answer now | Notifications, workflows, data sync |

**Analogy:** sync is a **phone call** (both people must be free right now). Async is **WhatsApp** (send it now, they read it when they can).

# Distributed transactions: saga and outbox

**Problem:** "Disburse a loan" touches loan-service (mark it disbursed), collection-service (create the ledger entry) and notification-service. Each has **its own database**, so there is no single ACID transaction.

**Two-phase commit (2PC)** locks everything across services. It is slow and fragile, so microservices avoid it.

## Saga: a chain of local transactions plus compensations

Each step commits locally and triggers the next. If a step fails, earlier steps are **undone with compensating actions**.

```
1. loan-service:        mark loan APPROVED        (compensate: mark CANCELLED)
2. collection-service:  create disbursement entry (compensate: post a REVERSAL entry)
3. payment-service:     send money to bKash       (if this fails → run the compensations for 2, then 1)
```
**Analogy:** booking a trip: flight, then hotel, then car. If the car booking fails, you cancel the hotel and the flight. You can't "roll back time", but you can **undo with new actions**.

Two styles:
- **Choreography:** each service listens for events and reacts (no central boss). Simple for 2–3 steps, but hard to follow for many.
- **Orchestration:** a central orchestrator tells each service what to do next. Easier to see the whole flow (tools: Camunda, Temporal).

## Transactional outbox: never lose an event

**Problem:** you save the loan to the DB **and** publish a Kafka event. If the app crashes between the two, the DB says "approved" but nobody was told (or the reverse).

```java
@Transactional
public void approve(Long loanId) {
    Loan loan = loans.findById(loanId).orElseThrow();
    loan.approve();
    outbox.save(new OutboxEvent("LoanApproved", loanId, toJson(loan)));   // SAME transaction
}

@Scheduled(fixedDelay = 1000)
public void publishOutbox() {
    for (OutboxEvent e : outbox.findTop100ByPublishedFalseOrderById()) {
        kafka.send("loan-events", e.getAggregateId().toString(), e.getPayload());
        e.markPublished();
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- The business change and the event row are written in **one local DB transaction**: both or neither.
- A separate poller (or **Debezium CDC**, which reads the DB log) publishes the unpublished events to Kafka.
- If publishing fails, it retries later. This gives **at-least-once** delivery, so consumers must be **idempotent** (e.g. store processed event IDs and skip duplicates).
- **Analogy:** instead of trying to mail a letter at the exact moment you write it in your diary, you write "to send: letter X" in the diary itself. An assistant checks the diary and mails everything marked "to send".

# Configuring SecurityFilterChain

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity                                   // enables @PreAuthorize
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())                                    // stateless token API
            .cors(Customizer.withDefaults())                                 // use the CorsConfigurationSource bean
            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/health/**", "/v3/api-docs/**", "/swagger-ui/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/api/v1/loans/**").hasAnyRole("OFFICER", "MANAGER")
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/v1/loans/*/approve").hasRole("MANAGER")
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()));    // validate JWT bearer tokens
        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    CorsConfigurationSource corsConfigurationSource() {
        CorsConfiguration c = new CorsConfiguration();
        c.setAllowedOrigins(List.of("https://app.pksf.org.bd"));
        c.setAllowedMethods(List.of("GET", "POST", "PUT", "PATCH", "DELETE"));
        c.setAllowedHeaders(List.of("Authorization", "Content-Type"));
        UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource src = new UrlBasedCorsConfigurationSource();
        src.registerCorsConfiguration("/api/**", c);
        return src;
    }
}
```

**Line by line**
- `csrf.disable()`: OK **only** for stateless APIs that use `Authorization` headers. Keep CSRF on if you use session cookies.
- `STATELESS`: never create an HTTP session. Every request must carry its token.
- `requestMatchers(...).permitAll()`: public endpoints (health, docs).
- `hasRole("MANAGER")`: checks for the authority `ROLE_MANAGER` (Spring adds the `ROLE_` prefix). `hasAuthority("loan:approve")` checks an exact string.
- **Rules are checked top to bottom, and the first match wins.** Put specific rules first and `anyRequest()` last.
- `oauth2ResourceServer(...jwt...)`: adds the filter that reads `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, verifies the signature and expiry, and builds the Authentication.
- CORS: only our frontend's origin may call the API from a browser. **Never use `*` together with credentials.**

## Method-level security

```java
@Service
public class LoanService {

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('MANAGER')")
    public void approve(Long loanId) { ... }

    @PreAuthorize("hasRole('OFFICER') and @poGuard.canAccess(#poId, authentication)")
    public List<LoanDto> loansOfPo(Long poId) { ... }

    @PostAuthorize("returnObject.poCode == authentication.token.claims['po_code']")
    public LoanDto get(Long id) { ... }
}
```
- `@PreAuthorize`: checked **before** the method runs. `#poId` refers to the method parameter, and `@poGuard` calls a bean.
- `@PostAuthorize`: checked **after**, using the return value. It is useful for ownership rules ("officers see only their own PO's loans").
- This defends against **broken access control**, OWASP's #1 risk. The URL rule says "officers can read loans", and the method rule says "**only loans of their own PO**".

# JWT (JSON Web Token)

A **JWT** is a signed token that carries claims (facts) about the user. The server can verify it **without a database lookup**.

**Analogy:** a festival wristband. Security checks the official stamp (the signature) and the date printed on it (the expiry). They don't need to phone the ticket office for every person.

## Structure: `header.payload.signature` (each part Base64URL-encoded)

```
eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiJ9 . eyJzdWIiOiJyYWhpbSIsInJvbGVzIjpbIk9GRklDRVIiXSwiZXhwIjoxNzMxNjQwMDAwfQ . kX9a...signature
```
```json
// Header
{ "alg": "RS256", "typ": "JWT", "kid": "key-2025-11" }
// Payload (claims)
{ "sub": "rahim", "iss": "https://auth.pksf.org.bd/realms/pksf",
  "aud": "loan-api", "exp": 1731640000, "iat": 1731639700,
  "roles": ["OFFICER"], "po_code": "PO-07" }
```
- `sub` = subject (the user), `iss` = issuer (who created it), `aud` = audience (who it is for), `exp` = expiry time, `iat` = issued at.
- **The payload is only encoded, NOT encrypted.** Anyone can read it (try jwt.io). **Never put passwords or sensitive data in it.**
- The **signature** proves nobody changed it. Change one character of the payload and the signature check fails.

## Signing algorithms
- **HS256 (symmetric):** the same secret signs and verifies. Every service that verifies must know the secret.
- **RS256 / ES256 (asymmetric):** the auth server signs with its **private key**, and services verify with the **public key** (published at a JWKS URL). This is the better choice for microservices and Keycloak.

## Validating in Spring (resource server)

```yaml
spring:
  security:
    oauth2:
      resourceserver:
        jwt:
          issuer-uri: https://auth.pksf.org.bd/realms/pksf
```
- With just this, Spring downloads the public keys (JWKS) from the issuer and checks **the signature, `exp`, `nbf` and `iss`** on every request.

## Hand-made JWT filter (to understand the idea; prefer the built-in resource server)

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class JwtAuthFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
    private final JwtService jwt;

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res, FilterChain chain)
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        String header = req.getHeader("Authorization");
        if (header != null && header.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            String token = header.substring(7);
            try {
                Claims c = jwt.parseAndVerify(token);                    // checks signature + expiry
                List<SimpleGrantedAuthority> auths = ((List<String>) c.get("roles")).stream()
                        .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r)).toList();
                var auth = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(c.getSubject(), null, auths);
                SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth);
            } catch (JwtException e) {
                res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);       // invalid/expired -> 401
                return;
            }
        }
        chain.doFilter(req, res);                                        // continue to the next filter
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `OncePerRequestFilter`: guaranteed to run exactly once per request.
- `substring(7)`: removes `"Bearer "` (7 characters).
- `parseAndVerify`: if the signature is wrong or the token has expired, it throws, and we return 401.
- We turn the roles claim into `GrantedAuthority` objects and store the Authentication in the SecurityContext, so `hasRole` checks work later.
- `chain.doFilter`: **always** pass the request on (unless rejecting it), or the request stops here.

## Access token + refresh token
- **Access token:** short-lived (5–15 min), sent with every API call.
- **Refresh token:** long-lived, used **only** to get a new access token from the auth server. It can be revoked.
- Why? A JWT **can't be revoked easily** before it expires (the server doesn't track it). Short lifetimes limit the damage if one is stolen.
- **Analogy:** the access token is a day pass. The refresh token is your membership card, which you show at the desk each morning to get a new day pass. If the card is cancelled, no more day passes.

## Where should the browser store tokens?
- `localStorage`: easy, but **readable by XSS** scripts.
- **HttpOnly Secure SameSite cookie** (or the BFF pattern, where the server keeps the tokens): JavaScript can't read it. This is the safer choice.
- In memory (a JS variable) for the access token plus silent refresh is a common SPA compromise.

# Data protection and auditing

## Protect data in transit and at rest
- **In transit:** HTTPS/TLS everywhere, including between services and to the DB (`sslmode=require`).
- **At rest:** disk/DB encryption, plus **column encryption** for very sensitive fields (NID, bank accounts).
- **Secrets:** never in Git. Use environment variables, Kubernetes Secrets, Vault.

## Column encryption with a JPA AttributeConverter

```java
@Converter
public class EncryptedStringConverter implements AttributeConverter<String, String> {
    private final AesGcmCipher cipher;                     // key loaded from a secret store
    public EncryptedStringConverter(AesGcmCipher cipher) { this.cipher = cipher; }

    @Override public String convertToDatabaseColumn(String plain) {
        return plain == null ? null : cipher.encrypt(plain);     // Java -> DB
    }
    @Override public String convertToEntityAttribute(String encrypted) {
        return encrypted == null ? null : cipher.decrypt(encrypted);  // DB -> Java
    }
}

@Entity
public class Member {
    @Convert(converter = EncryptedStringConverter.class)
    private String nationalId;
}
```
**Line by line**
- `AttributeConverter<EntityType, DbType>`: JPA calls it automatically on every save and load.
- The DB only ever stores ciphertext. A stolen backup doesn't reveal NIDs.
- **AES-GCM** is authenticated encryption: it detects tampering. Keep the key outside the DB.
- Trade-off: you can't search encrypted columns with LIKE. Store a separate **hash** column for exact lookups.

## Masking in responses and logs
```java
public static String maskPhone(String p) {           // 01712345678 -> 017*****678
    return p.substring(0, 3) + "*****" + p.substring(p.length() - 3);
}
```
- Show only what the user needs. Logs must **never** contain passwords, tokens or full NIDs.

## Audit trail: who changed what, and when

```java
@Entity
@Audited                                   // Hibernate Envers: keeps every version of the row
public class Loan { ... }
```
```java
AuditReader reader = AuditReaderFactory.get(entityManager);
List<Number> revisions = reader.getRevisions(Loan.class, loanId);
Loan oldVersion = reader.find(Loan.class, loanId, revisions.get(0));
```
**Line by line**
- `@Audited`: Envers creates a `loan_aud` table plus a `revinfo` table. Every insert/update/delete stores a snapshot with a revision number and timestamp.
- Add a custom `RevisionEntity` to record the **username** (from the SecurityContext).
- `AuditReader`: query history, e.g. "what was this loan's outstanding on 1 March?"
- Combine it with Spring Data `@CreatedBy/@LastModifiedBy` (see Entity Mapping) and an **immutable ledger** for money movements.
- **Analogy:** CCTV for your data. You can rewind and see who did what.

**Also mention in a viva:** least-privilege DB users, MFA for admins, rate limiting on login/OTP, account lockout, regular dependency scanning, backups with tested restores, and following Bangladesh's data-protection rules.

# Caching patterns and Spring Cache with Redis

## The patterns

| Pattern | How | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| **Cache-aside (lazy loading)** | The app checks the cache; on a miss, reads the DB and stores the result | Check your fridge; if empty, go to the shop and stock the fridge |
| **Write-through** | Write to the cache **and** the DB together | Update both your notebook and the office register at once |
| **Write-behind** | Write to the cache, flush to the DB later | Risky for money: data can be lost |
| **Read-through** | The cache library loads from the DB itself on a miss | The fridge restocks itself |

**Cache-aside is the most common**, and it is what Spring's `@Cacheable` does.

## Manual cache-aside

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class PoService {
    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final PoRepository repo;
    private final ObjectMapper json;

    public PoDto get(Long id) throws JsonProcessingException {
        String key = "po:" + id;
        String cached = redis.opsForValue().get(key);                // 1. try the cache
        if (cached != null) return json.readValue(cached, PoDto.class);

        PoDto dto = PoDto.from(repo.findById(id).orElseThrow());     // 2. miss -> DB
        redis.opsForValue().set(key, json.writeValueAsString(dto),
                                Duration.ofMinutes(30));             // 3. store with a TTL
        return dto;
    }

    public void update(Long id, PoRequest req) {
        repo.save(...);
        redis.delete("po:" + id);                                    // 4. invalidate on write
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- Step 1: a hit returns immediately. A miss returns `null`.
- Step 2: read the source of truth, the database.
- Step 3: `Duration.ofMinutes(30)`: a **TTL** means stale data fixes itself eventually.
- Step 4: on update, **delete** the key (don't update it). The next read reloads fresh data. Deleting avoids race conditions where two writers leave the cache holding the older value.

## The same thing with Spring Cache annotations

```yaml
spring:
  data:
    redis:
      host: localhost
      port: 6379
  cache:
    type: redis
    redis:
      time-to-live: 30m
```
```java
@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class App {}

@Service
public class PoService {

    @Cacheable(value = "po", key = "#id", unless = "#result == null")
    public PoDto get(Long id) { return PoDto.from(repo.findById(id).orElseThrow()); }

    @CachePut(value = "po", key = "#id")              // always run, and refresh the cache with the result
    public PoDto update(Long id, PoRequest req) { ... return dto; }

    @CacheEvict(value = "po", key = "#id")
    public void delete(Long id) { repo.deleteById(id); }

    @CacheEvict(value = "po", allEntries = true)      // clear the whole "po" cache
    public void reloadAll() {}
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@EnableCaching`: turns on the caching proxy.
- `@Cacheable(value = "po", key = "#id")`: the Redis key becomes `po::7`. If it is present, the method is **not executed**.
- `unless = "#result == null"`: don't cache empty results.
- `@CachePut`: runs the method every time and stores the new value.
- `@CacheEvict`: removes the entry when the data changes.
- The cached objects must be serializable. Configure a JSON serializer (`GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer`) via a `RedisCacheConfiguration` bean so that the values in Redis are readable.
- Proxy rule again: calling a `@Cacheable` method **from the same class** skips the cache.

## Common problems

| Problem | Meaning | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| **Stale data** | The cache shows an old value | TTL + evict on write |
| **Cache stampede** | A hot key expires and 1,000 requests hit the DB at once | A lock so that only one request reloads (`@Cacheable(sync = true)`), early refresh |
| **Cache penetration** | Requests for IDs that don't exist always miss | Cache "null" briefly, a Bloom filter |
| **Avalanche** | Many keys expire at the same moment | Add random jitter to TTLs |

**What to cache:** read-heavy, rarely changing data (PO lists, loan products, configuration, dashboard aggregates).
**What not to cache:** live balances where you need exact, current values. Read those from the DB inside a transaction.

# Async JavaScript and the event loop

JavaScript runs on **one thread**. Slow work (network, timers) is handed off, and its callback runs later. The **event loop** decides when.

**Analogy:** a single waiter in a restaurant. He takes your order (starts the request), gives it to the kitchen, and serves other tables meanwhile. When the kitchen rings the bell, he brings your food. He never stands frozen at the kitchen door.

## Promises and async/await

```js
// Promise chain
fetch("/api/v1/loans/7")
  .then(res => {
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return res.json();
  })
  .then(loan => console.log(loan.outstanding))
  .catch(err => console.error("Failed:", err.message));

// Same with async/await (easier to read)
async function loadLoan(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/v1/loans/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    const loan = await res.json();
    return loan;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed:", err.message);
    throw err;
  }
}

// Run in parallel
const [member, loans] = await Promise.all([getMember(42), getLoans(42)]);
```
**Line by line**
- A **Promise** is a placeholder for a future value: *pending → fulfilled or rejected*.
- `fetch` does **not** reject on 404/500. It only rejects on network failure. **Always check `res.ok`.**
- `res.json()` also returns a promise, because the body is read asynchronously.
- `await` pauses **this function** (not the whole page) until the promise settles. It can only be used inside `async` functions (or at module top level).
- `try/catch` handles rejected promises with `await`.
- `Promise.all`: start both requests together and wait for both. If any one fails, the whole thing rejects (`Promise.allSettled` gives every result).

## The event loop order (classic MCQ)

```js
console.log("1");
setTimeout(() => console.log("2"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("3"));
console.log("4");
// Output: 1, 4, 3, 2
```
**Why**
1. Synchronous code runs first: `1`, `4`.
2. Then **all microtasks** (promise callbacks): `3`.
3. Then the next **macrotask** (setTimeout, I/O): `2`.
- So even `setTimeout(..., 0)` waits until the current code and every pending promise callback have finished.

**Analogy:** the waiter finishes the current table (sync code), then handles the "quick requests" queue (microtasks: promises), then picks up the next big order from the "normal" queue (macrotasks: timers).

# Axios instance with interceptors

Instead of repeating the base URL, token and error handling in every component, create **one API client**.

```ts
// src/api/client.ts
import axios from "axios";

export const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL,     // e.g. https://api.pksf.org.bd/api/v1
  timeout: 15000,
});

// Before every request: attach the token
api.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  const token = authStore.getToken();
  if (token) config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  return config;
});

// After every response: handle errors in one place
api.interceptors.response.use(
  (response) => response,
  async (error) => {
    if (error.response?.status === 401) {
      authStore.logout();                    // token expired or invalid
      window.location.href = "/login";
    }
    if (error.response?.status === 403) {
      toast.error("You do not have permission");
    }
    return Promise.reject(error);            // let the caller handle it too
  }
);

// Usage
export const getLoan = (id: number) => api.get<Loan>(`/loans/${id}`).then(r => r.data);
export const createMember = (m: MemberRequest) => api.post<Member>("/members", m).then(r => r.data);
```
**Line by line**
- `axios.create`: a pre-configured instance. Every call uses the base URL and timeout.
- `import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL`: an environment variable (Vite), so dev and prod use different URLs.
- **Request interceptor:** runs before each request and adds the `Authorization` header automatically.
- **Response interceptor:** the first function handles success, the second handles errors. A 401 sends the user to login; a 403 shows a message.
- `return Promise.reject(error)`: keeps the error flowing so that the component can show its own message.
- `api.get<Loan>`: a TypeScript generic, so `r.data` is typed as `Loan`.
- Unlike `fetch`, axios **rejects on 4xx/5xx** and parses JSON automatically.

**Analogy:** interceptors are a security desk in the lobby. Every outgoing visitor gets a badge (the token) and every returning visitor is checked, so each office doesn't need its own desk.

# TypeScript essentials

**TypeScript** = JavaScript + **types**. The compiler catches mistakes before the code runs.
**Analogy:** a spell-checker for your code. It underlines `loan.outstnding` before your users ever see the bug.

```ts
// Basic types
let count: number = 5;
let name: string = "Rahim";
let active: boolean = true;
let ids: number[] = [1, 2, 3];

// Interface / type: the shape of an object
interface Member {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  phone?: string;                    // optional
  readonly nationalId: string;       // can't be changed after creation
}

// Union + literal types
type LoanStatus = "PENDING" | "ACTIVE" | "CLOSED";
let s: LoanStatus = "ACTIVE";
// s = "DONE";                       // compile error

// Function types
function totalOutstanding(loans: Loan[]): number {
  return loans.reduce((sum, l) => sum + l.outstanding, 0);
}

// Generics: reusable and type-safe
interface Page<T> { content: T[]; totalElements: number; page: number; }
async function fetchPage<T>(url: string): Promise<Page<T>> {
  const res = await api.get<Page<T>>(url);
  return res.data;
}
const members = await fetchPage<Member>("/members");   // members.content is Member[]

// Narrowing
function show(v: string | number) {
  if (typeof v === "string") return v.toUpperCase();   // TS knows v is a string here
  return v.toFixed(2);                                   // and a number here
}

// Utility types
type MemberForm = Omit<Member, "id">;          // everything except id
type MemberPatch = Partial<Member>;            // all fields optional
type MemberCard = Pick<Member, "name" | "phone">;
type ById = Record<number, Member>;            // { [id]: Member }
```
**Line by line**
- `phone?`: may be missing (`string | undefined`).
- `readonly`: assignment after creation is an error.
- **Literal union** `"PENDING" | "ACTIVE" | "CLOSED"`: only these exact strings are allowed. It works like a Java enum.
- `Page<T>`: one interface reused for members, loans, anything.
- **Narrowing:** after a `typeof` check, TS knows the exact type in each branch.
- `Omit`, `Partial`, `Pick`, `Record`: build new types from existing ones instead of copying fields.

**interface vs type:** both describe object shapes. `interface` can be extended or merged, while `type` can also express unions/intersections. Teams usually pick one style; either is fine.

**`any` vs `unknown`:** `any` turns type checking off (avoid it). `unknown` forces you to check the type before using the value (safer).

# Components, props and state

**Analogy:** components are LEGO bricks. **Props** are the settings you give a brick from outside (its colour). **State** is the brick's own memory (is the light on?).

```tsx
type LoanCardProps = { loanNo: string; outstanding: number; onRepay: (amount: number) => void };

function LoanCard({ loanNo, outstanding, onRepay }: LoanCardProps) {
  const [amount, setAmount] = useState<number>(0);          // local state

  return (
    <div className="card">
      <h3>{loanNo}</h3>
      <p>Outstanding: {outstanding.toLocaleString("en-BD")}</p>
      {outstanding === 0 ? <span>Closed</span> : (
        <>
          <input type="number" value={amount}
                 onChange={e => setAmount(Number(e.target.value))} />
          <button disabled={amount <= 0} onClick={() => onRepay(amount)}>Repay</button>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

function LoanList({ loans }: { loans: Loan[] }) {
  const [paid, setPaid] = useState<Record<string, number>>({});

  const handleRepay = (loanNo: string, amount: number) =>
    setPaid(prev => ({ ...prev, [loanNo]: (prev[loanNo] ?? 0) + amount }));

  if (loans.length === 0) return <p>No loans found.</p>;

  return (
    <ul>
      {loans.map(l => (
        <li key={l.loanNo}>
          <LoanCard loanNo={l.loanNo} outstanding={l.outstanding}
                    onRepay={amt => handleRepay(l.loanNo, amt)} />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

**Line by line**
- A component is a **function returning JSX**. JSX looks like HTML but compiles to `React.createElement(...)` calls.
- `{ loanNo, outstanding, onRepay }`: props are destructured. **Props are read-only.** A child never changes its props.
- `useState(0)`: returns `[currentValue, setter]`. Calling the setter **schedules a re-render** with the new value.
- `{expression}`: put JavaScript inside JSX.
- `cond ? <A/> : <B/>`: conditional rendering (or `cond && <A/>`).
- `<>...</>`: a **Fragment** groups elements without adding an extra `<div>`.
- `value={amount}` + `onChange`: a **controlled input**, where React state is the single source of truth.
- `onRepay`: a **callback prop**. The child tells the parent "something happened", which is how data flows **up**.
- `setPaid(prev => ({...prev, ...}))`: **functional update** + **new object**. Never mutate state (`paid[x] = 1` won't re-render). The `prev =>` form guarantees you build on the latest value.
- `key={l.loanNo}`: a stable unique key lets React match list items between renders. **Don't use the array index** if items can be reordered or removed.

**Data flow:** props go **down**, events go **up** (one-way data flow). When two siblings need the same data, **lift the state up** to their common parent.

# useEffect

`useEffect` runs **side effects** after render: fetching data, subscriptions, timers, changing the document title.

**Analogy:** after you rearrange your room (render), you might need to do follow-up chores (effects), like plugging the lamp back in. And before you rearrange again, you unplug it (cleanup).

```tsx
function MemberDetails({ memberId }: { memberId: number }) {
  const [member, setMember] = useState<Member | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();          // lets us cancel the request
    setLoading(true);

    api.get<Member>(`/members/${memberId}`, { signal: controller.signal })
       .then(r => { setMember(r.data); setError(null); })
       .catch(e => { if (!axios.isCancel(e)) setError("Could not load member"); })
       .finally(() => setLoading(false));

    return () => controller.abort();                   // CLEANUP: cancel if memberId changes or we unmount
  }, [memberId]);                                      // re-run only when memberId changes

  if (loading) return <p>Loading…</p>;
  if (error) return <p role="alert">{error}</p>;
  return <h2>{member?.name}</h2>;
}
```

**Line by line**
- The effect function runs **after** React paints the screen.
- **Dependency array `[memberId]`**: re-run the effect only when `memberId` changes.
  - `[]`: run once after the first render (mount).
  - No array at all: run after **every** render (usually a bug).
- The **cleanup** function (`return () => ...`) runs before the next effect run and on unmount. Here it cancels the old request, so a slow old response can't overwrite newer data (a **race condition**).
- Three states (`loading`, `error`, `data`): always handle all three in the UI.
- In development, React **StrictMode** runs effects twice on mount, deliberately, to expose missing cleanups.

**Common mistakes**
- Missing dependencies: the effect uses stale values. Follow the ESLint `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` rule.
- Objects or functions in the dependency array that are recreated every render: an infinite loop.
- Using an effect to compute derived data: just compute it during render instead (`const total = items.reduce(...)`).
- In real apps, use **TanStack Query** for server data instead of hand-written fetch effects (see the React Ecosystem topics).

# useRef, useMemo, useCallback, Context and useReducer

## useRef: a box that survives re-renders without causing them
```tsx
function SearchBox() {
  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
  const renders = useRef(0);
  renders.current++;                                   // changing .current does NOT re-render

  return <>
    <input ref={inputRef} />
    <button onClick={() => inputRef.current?.focus()}>Focus</button>
  </>;
}
```
- `ref={inputRef}`: React puts the real DOM element in `inputRef.current`, for focusing, scrolling or measuring.
- It also stores mutable values (timer IDs, previous values) that shouldn't trigger a render.

## useMemo and useCallback: remember results between renders
```tsx
function Portfolio({ loans, onSelect }: { loans: Loan[]; onSelect: (id: number) => void }) {
  const total = useMemo(
    () => loans.reduce((s, l) => s + l.outstanding, 0),   // recalculated only when loans changes
    [loans]);

  const handleClick = useCallback((id: number) => onSelect(id), [onSelect]);  // same function each render

  return <LoanTable loans={loans} onRowClick={handleClick} total={total} />;
}
const LoanTable = React.memo(LoanTableImpl);   // skip re-render if the props are the same
```
- `useMemo(fn, deps)`: caches a **computed value**.
- `useCallback(fn, deps)`: caches a **function** so that its identity stays the same, which matters when it is passed to a `React.memo` child or used as an effect dependency.
- `React.memo`: re-renders the child only if its props changed (shallow comparison).
- **Don't overuse them.** They cost memory and complexity. Use them for expensive calculations or to stop real re-render problems you have measured.
- **Analogy:** useMemo is writing a long calculation's answer on a sticky note, and only redoing it when the inputs change.

## Context: share data without "prop drilling"
```tsx
type Auth = { user: string | null; roles: string[]; logout: () => void };
const AuthContext = createContext<Auth | null>(null);

export function AuthProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, roles: [], logout: () => setUser(null) }), [user]);
  return <AuthContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AuthContext.Provider>;
}

export function useAuth() {                       // a custom hook for easy, safe access
  const ctx = useContext(AuthContext);
  if (!ctx) throw new Error("useAuth must be used inside AuthProvider");
  return ctx;
}

// Anywhere below the provider:
const { user, logout } = useAuth();
```
- **Prop drilling:** passing a prop through 5 components that don't need it, just to reach the 6th. Context solves this.
- `Provider value={...}`: every component inside can read it with `useContext`.
- `useMemo` on the value: otherwise a new object every render re-renders every consumer.
- Use it for app-wide, rarely changing data: the logged-in user, theme, language. For frequently changing global state, use a state library (Zustand/Redux).
- **Analogy:** context is the building's PA system. Instead of passing a note person to person, you announce it once and anyone listening hears it.

## useReducer: complex state with clear actions
```tsx
type State = { items: CartItem[]; total: number };
type Action = { type: "add"; item: CartItem } | { type: "remove"; id: number } | { type: "clear" };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "add":    return { items: [...state.items, action.item], total: state.total + action.item.amount };
    case "remove": {
      const items = state.items.filter(i => i.id !== action.id);
      return { items, total: items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.amount, 0) };
    }
    case "clear":  return { items: [], total: 0 };
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { items: [], total: 0 });
dispatch({ type: "add", item: { id: 1, amount: 500 } });
```
- The **reducer** is a pure function: (old state, action) → new state. All the update logic is in one place and easy to test.
- `dispatch(action)`: describe **what happened**, and the reducer decides how the state changes.
- Use it when the state has many related fields or the next state depends on complex rules. (Redux works on the same idea.)

## Rules of hooks
1. Call hooks only at the **top level** (not inside if/loops/nested functions). React relies on call order.
2. Call them only from React components or custom hooks (functions named `useSomething`).

# Custom hooks

A **custom hook** is a function starting with `use` that uses other hooks. It lets you **reuse logic** (not UI) across components.

**Analogy:** a recipe card you can hand to any cook. Every component that uses the hook gets its **own** copy of the state, just as each cook makes their own dish from the same recipe.

```tsx
// Debounce: wait until the user stops typing
function useDebounce<T>(value: T, delay = 400): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);              // the user typed again: cancel the old timer
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;
}

// Local storage-backed state
function useLocalStorage<T>(key: string, initial: T) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState<T>(() => {
    try { const raw = localStorage.getItem(key); return raw ? JSON.parse(raw) as T : initial; }
    catch { return initial; }
  });
  useEffect(() => {
    try { localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value)); } catch { /* storage unavailable */ }
  }, [key, value]);
  return [value, setValue] as const;
}

// Usage
function MemberSearch() {
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const query = useDebounce(text, 400);
  const [pageSize, setPageSize] = useLocalStorage("pageSize", 20);
  const { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ["members", query, pageSize],
                              queryFn: () => searchMembers(query, pageSize),
                              enabled: query.length >= 2 });
  return <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} placeholder="Search name/phone" />;
}
```
**Line by line**
- `useDebounce`: each keystroke resets a 400 ms timer. Only when the typing pauses does `debounced` update, so the API is called once instead of 10 times.
- The cleanup `clearTimeout(t)`: cancels the pending update when the value changes again.
- `useState(() => ...)`: **lazy initial state**. The function runs only on the first render, so we don't read localStorage on every render.
- `try/catch` around localStorage: it can throw in private mode or when blocked.
- `as const`: returns a typed tuple `[T, setter]`, like `useState`.
- `enabled: query.length >= 2`: don't search for one character.

# Routing and protected routes

**React Router** maps URLs to components without full page reloads, which makes the app an SPA (Single Page Application).

```tsx
// main.tsx
const router = createBrowserRouter([
  { path: "/login", element: <LoginPage /> },
  {
    element: <RequireAuth />,                       // guard for everything below
    children: [
      { path: "/", element: <Dashboard /> },
      { path: "/members", element: <MemberList /> },
      { path: "/members/:id", element: <MemberDetails /> },
      {
        element: <RequireRole role="MANAGER" />,
        children: [{ path: "/loans/approvals", element: <Approvals /> }],
      },
    ],
  },
  { path: "*", element: <NotFound /> },
]);
createRoot(document.getElementById("root")!).render(<RouterProvider router={router} />);

// RequireAuth.tsx
function RequireAuth() {
  const { user } = useAuth();
  const location = useLocation();
  if (!user) return <Navigate to="/login" replace state={{ from: location }} />;
  return <Outlet />;                                // render the matched child route
}

function RequireRole({ role }: { role: string }) {
  const { roles } = useAuth();
  return roles.includes(role) ? <Outlet /> : <p>403: Not allowed</p>;
}

// Reading params and navigating
function MemberDetails() {
  const { id } = useParams();                       // "/members/42" -> id = "42"
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  return <button onClick={() => navigate("/members")}>Back</button>;
}
```
**Line by line**
- `createBrowserRouter([...])`: the route table.
- A route with only `element` and `children` (no path) is a **layout/guard route**. Its element wraps all its children.
- `RequireAuth`: if nobody is logged in, `<Navigate>` redirects to /login. `state={{from}}` remembers where the user wanted to go, so we can send them back after login.
- `replace`: replaces the history entry, so "Back" doesn't return to the protected page.
- `<Outlet />`: "render the matching child route here".
- `:id` in the path → `useParams()` gives `{ id: "42" }` (always a **string**, so convert with `Number(id)`).
- `path: "*"`: catch-all for 404.
- **Important:** frontend guards are for **user experience only**. Anyone can edit browser code, so **the backend must enforce security** on every API call.

# Server state with TanStack Query

**Server state** (data from the API) is different from UI state: it is shared, can go stale, and needs loading/error handling, caching and refetching. **TanStack Query** (React Query) handles all of this.

**Analogy:** a smart personal assistant. You ask "get me the member list". They remember the answer, give it to you instantly next time, quietly check for updates in the background, and throw out the old copy when you tell them something changed.

```tsx
// main.tsx
const queryClient = new QueryClient({ defaultOptions: { queries: { staleTime: 30_000, retry: 1 } } });
<QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}><App /></QueryClientProvider>

// Reading data
function MemberList({ page }: { page: number }) {
  const { data, isPending, isError, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ["members", page],                       // cache key: changes -> new fetch
    queryFn: () => api.get<Page<Member>>(`/members?page=${page}`).then(r => r.data),
    placeholderData: keepPreviousData,                 // keep the old page visible while the next loads
  });

  if (isPending) return <Spinner />;
  if (isError) return <p role="alert">{(error as Error).message}</p>;
  return <MemberTable rows={data.content} />;
}

// Changing data
function NewMemberForm() {
  const qc = useQueryClient();
  const mutation = useMutation({
    mutationFn: (m: MemberRequest) => api.post<Member>("/members", m).then(r => r.data),
    onSuccess: () => {
      qc.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ["members"] }); // mark the list stale -> refetch
      toast.success("Member created");
    },
    onError: (e) => toast.error("Could not save"),
  });

  return <button disabled={mutation.isPending}
                 onClick={() => mutation.mutate({ name: "Rahim", phone: "01712345678" })}>
           {mutation.isPending ? "Saving…" : "Save"}
         </button>;
}
```
**Line by line**
- `QueryClient`: holds the cache. `staleTime: 30_000` means the data counts as fresh for 30 s (no refetch during that time).
- `queryKey: ["members", page]`: identifies the data. Different pages are cached separately. When the key changes, the data is fetched automatically.
- `queryFn`: how to fetch it. It must return a promise or throw on error.
- `isPending` / `isError` / `data`: you get the loading and error states for free.
- `keepPreviousData`: no flicker to a spinner when paging.
- `useMutation`: for POST/PUT/DELETE. `mutate(variables)` runs it.
- `invalidateQueries(["members"])`: every query whose key starts with "members" is marked stale and refetched, so the list shows the new member.
- `mutation.isPending`: disable the button to prevent double submits.

Also free: deduplication (10 components asking for the same key means 1 request), refetch on window focus, retries, and pagination/infinite queries.

# Forms with React Hook Form + Zod

- **React Hook Form (RHF):** manages form state with minimal re-renders.
- **Zod:** defines a **validation schema** once, and TypeScript types are generated from it.

**Analogy:** Zod is the official form rulebook ("phone must be 11 digits"). RHF is the clerk who collects the form, checks it against the rulebook and points out each mistake.

```tsx
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { z } from "zod";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";

const memberSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().trim().min(2, "Name is too short").max(100),
  phone: z.string().regex(/^01[3-9]\d{8}$/, "Invalid BD mobile number"),
  nationalId: z.string().regex(/^(\d{10}|\d{13}|\d{17})$/, "NID must be 10, 13 or 17 digits"),
  loanAmount: z.coerce.number().min(1000, "Minimum 1,000").max(500000),
});
type MemberForm = z.infer<typeof memberSchema>;            // the TS type comes FROM the schema

function MemberFormPage() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors, isSubmitting }, reset } =
    useForm<MemberForm>({ resolver: zodResolver(memberSchema) });

  const onSubmit = async (values: MemberForm) => {
    await api.post("/members", values);
    reset();                                               // clear the form after success
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)} noValidate>
      <label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
      <input id="name" {...register("name")} aria-invalid={!!errors.name} />
      {errors.name && <span role="alert">{errors.name.message}</span>}

      <label htmlFor="phone">Phone</label>
      <input id="phone" {...register("phone")} />
      {errors.phone && <span role="alert">{errors.phone.message}</span>}

      <label htmlFor="amt">Loan amount</label>
      <input id="amt" type="number" {...register("loanAmount")} />
      {errors.loanAmount && <span role="alert">{errors.loanAmount.message}</span>}

      <button type="submit" disabled={isSubmitting}>{isSubmitting ? "Saving…" : "Save"}</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```
**Line by line**
- `z.object({...})`: the rules. Each field has its own validators and error messages.
- `z.coerce.number()`: HTML inputs always give strings, so coerce converts "5000" to 5000.
- `z.infer<typeof memberSchema>`: **one source of truth**. You never write the type separately, so it can't drift from the rules.
- `zodResolver(memberSchema)`: connects Zod to RHF.
- `{...register("name")}`: spreads `name`, `onChange`, `onBlur` and `ref` onto the input. RHF tracks the input **without** a React state per keystroke (uncontrolled), so it is fast.
- `handleSubmit(onSubmit)`: validates first and calls `onSubmit` only if everything is valid.
- `errors.name?.message`: the message from the schema.
- `aria-invalid` and `role="alert"`: screen readers announce the errors.
- `isSubmitting`: true while the async `onSubmit` runs, so the button is disabled (no double submits).
- **The server must validate again.** Client validation is for convenience, not security.

# UI libraries, tables and charts

Admin/MIS apps (like PKSF's) are mostly **forms, tables and dashboards**. Don't build everything from scratch.

| Need | Popular choices |
|---|---|
| Component kit | **MUI**, **Ant Design** (great for admin panels), Chakra, shadcn/ui |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS, CSS Modules, styled-components |
| Data tables | **TanStack Table**, MUI DataGrid, AG Grid |
| Charts | Recharts, Chart.js, ECharts |
| Dates | date-fns, Day.js |
| Global UI state | Zustand, Redux Toolkit |

```tsx
// Ant Design table with server-side pagination
import { Table } from "antd";

function LoanTable() {
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const { data, isFetching } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ["loans", page],
    queryFn: () => api.get<Page<Loan>>(`/loans?page=${page - 1}&size=20`).then(r => r.data),
  });

  return (
    <Table<Loan>
      rowKey="id"
      loading={isFetching}
      dataSource={data?.content}
      columns={[
        { title: "Loan No", dataIndex: "loanNo" },
        { title: "Member", dataIndex: "memberName" },
        { title: "Outstanding", dataIndex: "outstanding", align: "right",
          render: (v: number) => v.toLocaleString("en-BD", { minimumFractionDigits: 2 }) },
      ]}
      pagination={{ current: page, pageSize: 20, total: data?.totalElements, onChange: setPage }}
    />
  );
}
```
**Line by line**
- `rowKey="id"`: a unique key per row (the same idea as React `key`).
- `columns`: what to show. `render` formats the cell (money with 2 decimals, right-aligned, which is easier to compare).
- `page - 1`: Ant Design pages start at 1, while Spring pages start at 0. A classic off-by-one bug.
- **Server-side pagination**: only 20 rows travel over the network, never 100,000.
- `total` from the API lets the table show the right number of pages.

**Charts tip:** for a PAR trend, use a line chart over months. To compare POs, use a bar chart. Always label units (BDT, %).

# JUnit 5 basics

```java
class InterestCalculatorTest {

    private FlatInterest calc;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {                                     // runs before EACH test: fresh object
        calc = new FlatInterest(new BigDecimal("0.12"));
    }

    @Test
    @DisplayName("12% flat on 10,000 for 12 months = 1,200.00")
    void calculatesFlatInterest() {
        // Arrange
        BigDecimal principal = new BigDecimal("10000");
        // Act
        BigDecimal interest = calc.calculate(principal, 12);
        // Assert
        assertEquals(new BigDecimal("1200.00"), interest);
    }

    @Test
    void rejectsNegativePrincipal() {
        IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
                () -> calc.calculate(new BigDecimal("-1"), 12));
        assertTrue(ex.getMessage().contains("positive"));
    }

    @ParameterizedTest
    @CsvSource({ "10000, 6, 600.00", "5000, 12, 600.00", "0.01, 1, 0.00" })
    void manyCases(BigDecimal p, int months, BigDecimal expected) {
        assertEquals(expected, calc.calculate(p, months));
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@Test`: marks a test method. JUnit 5 test classes/methods don't need to be public.
- `@BeforeEach`: setup before every test, so **tests don't share state** and can run in any order. (`@BeforeAll` runs once, on a static method.)
- `@DisplayName`: a readable name in reports.
- **AAA (Arrange-Act-Assert):** a clear 3-part structure for every test. (Also called Given-When-Then.)
- `assertEquals(expected, actual)`: **expected comes first**. Note: BigDecimal `equals` checks the scale too, so `1200.00` ≠ `1200`. Use `compareTo` or AssertJ's `isEqualByComparingTo`.
- `assertThrows`: checks that the code throws, and returns the exception so that you can inspect the message.
- `@ParameterizedTest` + `@CsvSource`: one test method, many input rows. Great for edge cases (zero, tiny amounts, rounding).

**AssertJ (more readable, included in spring-boot-starter-test):**
```java
assertThat(interest).isEqualByComparingTo("1200.00");
assertThat(members).hasSize(3).extracting(Member::getName).contains("Rahim");
```

**A good unit test is FIRST:** Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-validating, Timely.

# Mockito: unit testing a service

A **mock** is a fake object that you control. It lets you test one class **in isolation**, without a real DB, SMS gateway or network.

**Analogy:** a flight simulator. Pilots practise emergencies without risking a real plane, and the instructor decides exactly what "goes wrong".

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class RepaymentServiceTest {

    @Mock LoanRepository loans;               // fake
    @Mock LedgerRepository ledger;            // fake
    @Mock SmsSender sms;                      // fake
    @InjectMocks RepaymentService service;    // REAL class, with the mocks injected

    @Test
    void repayReducesOutstandingAndWritesLedger() {
        // Arrange
        Loan loan = new Loan(7L, new BigDecimal("5000.00"));
        when(loans.findById(7L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(loan));

        // Act
        service.repay(7L, new BigDecimal("1000.00"));

        // Assert: state
        assertThat(loan.getOutstanding()).isEqualByComparingTo("4000.00");

        // Assert: interactions
        ArgumentCaptor<LedgerEntry> captor = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(LedgerEntry.class);
        verify(ledger).save(captor.capture());
        assertThat(captor.getValue().getAmount()).isEqualByComparingTo("1000.00");
        verify(sms, times(1)).send(anyString(), contains("1000"));
    }

    @Test
    void repayMoreThanOutstandingFails() {
        when(loans.findById(7L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(new Loan(7L, new BigDecimal("500.00"))));

        assertThrows(BusinessRuleException.class,
                () -> service.repay(7L, new BigDecimal("1000.00")));

        verify(ledger, never()).save(any());          // nothing must be written
        verifyNoInteractions(sms);
    }

    @Test
    void unknownLoanThrowsNotFound() {
        when(loans.findById(99L)).thenReturn(Optional.empty());
        assertThrows(NotFoundException.class, () -> service.repay(99L, BigDecimal.TEN));
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)`: enables the Mockito annotations in JUnit 5. **No Spring context is started**, so the test runs in milliseconds.
- `@Mock`: creates a fake. Unstubbed methods return defaults (null, 0, empty Optional/collections).
- `@InjectMocks`: creates the real `RepaymentService` and passes the mocks into its constructor.
- `when(...).thenReturn(...)`: **stubbing**, i.e. "when asked for loan 7, return this loan".
- `verify(ledger).save(...)`: checks that the method **was called**. `times(1)`, `never()`, `atLeastOnce()`.
- `ArgumentCaptor`: grabs the actual argument passed in, so you can inspect it.
- `anyString()`, `any()`, `contains("1000")`: **argument matchers**. If you use a matcher for one argument, use matchers for all of them.
- `thenThrow(new RuntimeException())`: simulates failures (e.g. the SMS gateway is down).

**Mock vs stub vs spy:** a stub returns canned answers, a mock also verifies interactions, and a spy (`@Spy`) wraps a **real** object and overrides only some methods.

**Don't mock what you don't own** too deeply (e.g. complex library internals). And don't mock value objects (BigDecimal, DTOs): just create real ones.

# Testing controllers with @WebMvcTest

`@WebMvcTest` starts **only the web layer** (controllers, advice, filters, JSON conversion), not services or the DB. It is fast and focused.

```java
@WebMvcTest(MemberController.class)
class MemberControllerTest {

    @Autowired MockMvc mvc;                         // a simulated HTTP client, no real server
    @Autowired ObjectMapper json;
    @MockitoBean MemberService service;             // Spring Boot 3.4+ (older: @MockBean)

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "OFFICER")
    void getReturnsMember() throws Exception {
        when(service.get(1L)).thenReturn(new MemberResponse(1L, "Rahim", "01712345678", "Samity A"));

        mvc.perform(get("/api/v1/members/1"))
           .andExpect(status().isOk())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Rahim"))
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.phone").value("01712345678"));
    }

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "OFFICER")
    void createValidatesInput() throws Exception {
        var bad = new MemberRequest("", "123", "abc", LocalDate.now().plusDays(1), null);

        mvc.perform(post("/api/v1/members")
                    .with(csrf())
                    .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                    .content(json.writeValueAsString(bad)))
           .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors.name").exists())
           .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors.phone").exists());

        verifyNoInteractions(service);              // the service must not be called for invalid input
    }

    @Test
    void anonymousGets401() throws Exception {
        mvc.perform(get("/api/v1/members/1")).andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@WebMvcTest(MemberController.class)`: loads only this controller plus the MVC infrastructure.
- `MockMvc`: sends fake HTTP requests through the full Spring MVC pipeline (filters, validation, exception handlers), with no network.
- `@MockitoBean`: puts a Mockito mock **into the Spring context** in place of the real service.
- `@WithMockUser(roles = "OFFICER")`: pretends a user with that role is logged in (spring-security-test).
- `jsonPath("$.name")`: reads a field from the JSON response. `$` is the root.
- `.with(csrf())`: adds a CSRF token. It is needed if CSRF is enabled in the security config the test loads.
- The validation test proves that `@Valid` + `@RestControllerAdvice` return 400 with field errors, **and** that the service is never reached.
- The 401 test proves security is actually on. Always test the "not allowed" paths too.

# Repository and integration tests with Testcontainers

**Problem:** testing against H2 (an in-memory DB) differs from PostgreSQL: different SQL, types and behaviour. Tests pass, and production fails.
**Testcontainers** starts a **real PostgreSQL in Docker** for your tests and throws it away afterwards.

**Analogy:** rehearsing a play on the real stage instead of in your living room.

## @DataJpaTest with real Postgres

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)   // don't swap in H2
@Testcontainers
class LoanRepositoryTest {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection                                   // Boot 3.1+: auto-wires the datasource URL/user/password
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> pg = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @Autowired LoanRepository repo;
    @Autowired TestEntityManager em;

    @Test
    void findsOnlyActiveLoansOfSamity() {
        Samity s = em.persist(new Samity("Samity A"));
        Member m = em.persist(new Member("Rahim", s));
        em.persist(new Loan(m, new BigDecimal("5000"), LoanStatus.ACTIVE));
        em.persist(new Loan(m, new BigDecimal("3000"), LoanStatus.CLOSED));
        em.flush(); em.clear();                          // force SQL, then read fresh from the DB

        List<Loan> result = repo.activeInSamity(s.getId());

        assertThat(result).hasSize(1)
                          .first().extracting(Loan::getStatus).isEqualTo(LoanStatus.ACTIVE);
    }
}
```
**Line by line**
- `@DataJpaTest`: starts only JPA (entities, repositories, Flyway). **Each test runs in a transaction that is rolled back**, so the DB is clean for the next test.
- `Replace.NONE`: keep our real Postgres instead of the embedded default.
- `@Container static`: one container shared by all tests in the class (started once, which is faster).
- `@ServiceConnection`: Spring Boot reads the container's random port and credentials and configures the DataSource. No manual properties needed.
- `TestEntityManager.persist`: inserts test data directly.
- `flush(); clear();`: make sure the query really hits the DB and not Hibernate's first-level cache.
- Flyway migrations run against the container, so your **migrations are tested too**.

## Full integration test

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@Testcontainers
class RepaymentFlowIT {

    @Container @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> pg = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:16-alpine");

    @Autowired TestRestTemplate http;

    @Test
    void repaymentUpdatesOutstanding() {
        ResponseEntity<LoanResponse> before = http.getForEntity("/api/v1/loans/1", LoanResponse.class);
        http.postForEntity("/api/v1/loans/1/repayments", new RepaymentRequest(new BigDecimal("500")), Void.class);
        ResponseEntity<LoanResponse> after = http.getForEntity("/api/v1/loans/1", LoanResponse.class);

        assertThat(after.getBody().outstanding())
            .isEqualByComparingTo(before.getBody().outstanding().subtract(new BigDecimal("500")));
    }
}
```
- `@SpringBootTest(RANDOM_PORT)`: starts the **whole app** on a random port. It is slow, so keep these tests few and use them for critical flows.
- `TestRestTemplate`: a real HTTP client against the running app.
- Name integration tests `*IT` and run them with the Maven **Failsafe** plugin (`mvn verify`). Unit tests (`*Test`) run with Surefire (`mvn test`).

| Test type | Annotation | Starts | Speed |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit | none / `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension)` | Nothing | ms |
| Web slice | `@WebMvcTest` | MVC only | ~1 s |
| JPA slice | `@DataJpaTest` | JPA + DB | seconds |
| Full | `@SpringBootTest` | Everything | slowest |

# Frontend testing

| Tool | Job |
|---|---|
| **Vitest** (or Jest) | Test runner + assertions |
| **React Testing Library (RTL)** | Render components and interact like a **user** |
| **MSW (Mock Service Worker)** | Fake API responses at the network level |
| **Playwright / Cypress** | End-to-end tests in a real browser |

**RTL philosophy:** "Test what the user sees and does, not the implementation details." Find elements by **role, label or text**, not by CSS class.

```tsx
// MemberForm.test.tsx
import { render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import userEvent from "@testing-library/user-event";
import { describe, it, expect, vi } from "vitest";

describe("MemberForm", () => {
  it("shows a validation error for an invalid phone", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    render(<MemberForm onSave={vi.fn()} />);

    await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/phone/i), "123");
    await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /save/i }));

    expect(await screen.findByText(/invalid bd mobile/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  it("calls onSave with the form values", async () => {
    const user = userEvent.setup();
    const onSave = vi.fn();
    render(<MemberForm onSave={onSave} />);

    await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/name/i), "Rahim");
    await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/phone/i), "01712345678");
    await user.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: /save/i }));

    expect(onSave).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.objectContaining({ name: "Rahim", phone: "01712345678" }));
  });
});
```
**Line by line**
- `render(<MemberForm />)`: mounts the component in a simulated DOM (jsdom).
- `userEvent.setup()` + `user.type` / `user.click`: realistic typing and clicking (focus, key events).
- `getByLabelText(/phone/i)`: finds the input by its **label**. If this fails, your form isn't accessible either, which is a nice side benefit.
- `getByRole("button", { name: /save/i })`: finds the button the way a screen reader would.
- `findByText`: **async**, it waits for the element to appear (validation runs asynchronously).
- `vi.fn()`: a mock function that records its calls. `toHaveBeenCalledWith` checks the arguments.
- `getBy...` throws if the element is missing, `queryBy...` returns null (use it to assert something is absent), `findBy...` waits.

## MSW: mock the API
```ts
import { http, HttpResponse } from "msw";
import { setupServer } from "msw/node";

const server = setupServer(
  http.get("/api/v1/members/:id", ({ params }) =>
    HttpResponse.json({ id: Number(params.id), name: "Rahim" }))
);
beforeAll(() => server.listen());
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());
afterAll(() => server.close());
```
- The real axios/fetch code runs unchanged. MSW intercepts the request and returns fake data, so you test the actual data-fetching logic without a backend.

# Java output prediction practice

Cover the answers, predict the output, then read the explanation.

**1. String comparison**
```java
String a = "java";
String b = "ja" + "va";
String c = new String("java");
System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (a == c) + " " + a.equals(c));
```
**Answer:** `true false true`
- `"ja" + "va"` is concatenated **at compile time** into the literal "java", so it comes from the pool and `a == b`.
- `new String` creates a new object, so `==` is false while `equals` is true.

**2. Integer caching**
```java
Integer x = 127, y = 127, p = 128, q = 128;
System.out.println((x == y) + " " + (p == q));
```
**Answer:** `true false`. Integers from -128 to 127 are cached and 128 is not. Use `equals`.

**3. Pre/post increment**
```java
int i = 5;
int j = i++ + ++i;
System.out.println(i + " " + j);
```
**Answer:** `7 12`
- `i++` uses 5, then i becomes 6. `++i` makes i 7 and uses 7. j = 5 + 7 = 12.

**4. finally with return**
```java
static int test() {
    try { return 1; }
    finally { System.out.print("F "); }
}
System.out.println(test());
```
**Answer:** `F 1`. `finally` runs **before** the method actually returns, but the return value 1 was already decided.

**5. Static vs instance (method hiding)**
```java
class A { static String who() { return "A"; } String me() { return "A"; } }
class B extends A { static String who() { return "B"; } String me() { return "B"; } }
A obj = new B();
System.out.println(obj.who() + obj.me());
```
**Answer:** `AB`
- Static methods are **not overridden**; they are chosen by the **reference type** (A).
- Instance methods are overridden; they are chosen by the **actual object** (B).

**6. Char arithmetic**
```java
char ch = 'A';
System.out.println(ch + 1);
System.out.println((char) (ch + 1));
System.out.println("" + ch + 1);
```
**Answer:** `66`, `B`, `A1`
- `ch + 1` promotes the char to an int (65 + 1).
- The cast turns 66 back into the char 'B'.
- Starting with `""` makes it string concatenation, left to right.

**7. String concatenation order**
```java
System.out.println(1 + 2 + "3" + 4 + 5);
```
**Answer:** `3345`. Left to right: 1 + 2 = 3 (numbers), then "3" + "3" = "33", then "334", then "3345".

**8. Integer division and modulo**
```java
System.out.println(7 / 2 + " " + 7 % 3 + " " + -7 / 2 + " " + -7 % 3 + " " + 7.0 / 2);
```
**Answer:** `3 1 -3 -1 3.5`. Integer division truncates toward zero, and the remainder takes the sign of the dividend.

**9. Overloading resolution**
```java
static void f(Object o) { System.out.print("Object "); }
static void f(String s) { System.out.print("String "); }
f(null);
```
**Answer:** `String`. Java picks the **most specific** applicable overload, and String is more specific than Object.

**10. Exceptions in a loop**
```java
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3));
for (Integer n : list) if (n == 2) list.remove(n);
System.out.println(list);
```
**Answer:** in this exact case it prints `[1, 3]` without an exception, because removing the second-to-last element makes `hasNext()` return false before the check runs. In general, modifying a list during for-each throws `ConcurrentModificationException`. Use `removeIf`.

**11. Short-circuit**
```java
int k = 0;
if (k != 0 && 10 / k > 1) System.out.println("big");
System.out.println("safe");
```
**Answer:** `safe`. `&&` stops at the first false, so `10 / k` never runs (no ArithmeticException). With `&` it would throw.

**12. Ternary type promotion**
```java
Object o = true ? 1 : "one";
System.out.println(o.getClass().getSimpleName());
Object n = true ? 1 : 2.0;
System.out.println(n);
```
**Answer:** `Integer`, then `1.0`. In the second, both branches are numeric, so the int is promoted to double.
